265
00:09:44.856 --> 00:09:46.610
He's the first person to combine these technologies

266
00:09:46.610 --> 00:09:48.160
when studying sleep.

267
00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:49.960
There's a technology that can scan your eyes

268
00:09:49.960 --> 00:09:51.340
to see what you're looking at,

269
00:09:51.340 --> 00:09:52.253
and there's a technology that we all know about

270
00:09:52.253 --> 00:09:53.850
that can scan your brain.

271
00:09:53.850 --> 00:09:56.580
And so we put them together he gets tired of people,

272
00:09:56.580 --> 00:09:59.660
and they're not even that tired into this machine

273
00:09:59.660 --> 00:10:00.493
and he tracks them.

274
00:10:00.493 --> 00:10:01.450
What they're looking at and what's happening

275
00:10:01.450 --> 00:10:02.627
in their brains.

276
00:10:02.627 --> 00:10:05.310
And what he discovered is kind of mind blowing.

277
00:10:05.310 --> 00:10:06.143
You can be...

278
00:10:06.143 --> 00:10:07.514
As awake as you and I appear to be,

279
00:10:07.514 --> 00:10:10.540
you can be looking around you, you can be talking,

280
00:10:10.540 --> 00:10:13.610
but whole parts of your brain kind of gone to sleep.

281
00:10:13.610 --> 00:10:15.540
It's called local sleep cause it's local

282
00:10:15.540 --> 00:10:16.810
to one of the brain.

283
00:10:16.810 --> 00:10:19.260
So when we say I'm half asleep,

284
00:10:19.260 --> 00:10:20.850
it turns out that's not a metaphor, right?

285
00:10:20.850 --> 00:10:23.979
A lot of us are literally half asleep a lot of the time.

286
00:10:23.979 --> 00:10:25.900
And there's a lot of reasons why this is so important

287
00:10:25.900 --> 00:10:26.960
for our attention.

288
00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:28.529
One is as Professor Roxanne Prichard,

289
00:10:28.529 --> 00:10:30.220
University of Minneapolis,

290
00:10:30.220 --> 00:10:32.800
explain to me is that when you're sleeping

291
00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:35.790
your brain is repairing, it's healing itself.

292
00:10:35.790 --> 00:10:36.950
Throughout the day when you're awake

293
00:10:36.950 --> 00:10:39.190
metabolic waste builds up in your brain, right?

294
00:10:39.190 --> 00:10:41.180
She calls it brain cell poop.

295
00:10:41.180 --> 00:10:43.500
And when you're sleeping a watery fluid

296
00:10:43.500 --> 00:10:45.400
rinses through your brain and it carries

297
00:10:45.400 --> 00:10:47.520
this brain cell poop out of your brain

298
00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:50.200
down into your liver and eventually out of your body.

299
00:10:50.200 --> 00:10:53.230
If you don't sleep your brain doesn't clean out

300
00:10:53.230 --> 00:10:54.410
that metabolic waste.

301
00:10:54.410 --> 00:10:55.760
You know that feeling when you're almost hung...

302
00:10:55.760 --> 00:10:59.150
You feel almost hung over or clogged up when you are tired?

303
00:10:59.150 --> 00:11:01.560
Well, that's 'cause your brain is literally clogged up.

304
00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:03.510
So you can see that's one example, right?

305
00:11:03.510 --> 00:11:06.510
Of how we sleep much less.

306
00:11:06.510 --> 00:11:08.970
Think about how if you have a night where you don't sleep,

307
00:11:08.970 --> 00:11:11.040
the next day you're much more likely to be mindlessly

308
00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:12.840
scrolling through Tik Tok than a night where you

309
00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:14.230
had a really good night's sleep.

310
00:11:14.230 --> 00:11:16.313
So you can see that's one of many factors

311
00:11:16.313 --> 00:11:18.890
that are undermining our ability to focus.

312
00:11:18.890 --> 00:11:21.590
And then that combines with technologies

313
00:11:21.590 --> 00:11:22.640
that are currently designed...

314
00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:24.080
The technology doesn't have to work this way.

315
00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:26.420
But the technologies that are currently designed

316
00:11:26.420 --> 00:11:28.560
to hack and invade our attention as much as possible.

317
00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:31.940
You can see how that's such a kind of toxic combination.

318
00:11:31.940 --> 00:11:35.683
And I think for people who want to think about this,

319
00:11:36.820 --> 00:11:38.420
it's worth thinking about why that's such

320
00:11:38.420 --> 00:11:39.980
a toxic combination.

321
00:11:39.980 --> 00:11:43.040
And I would just say to anyone watching or listening,

322
00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:45.380
think about anything you've ever achieved in your life

323
00:11:45.380 --> 00:11:46.850
that you're proud of.

324
00:11:46.850 --> 00:11:49.080
Whether it's starting a business,

325
00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:51.790
being a good parent, learning to play the guitar.

326
00:11:51.790 --> 00:11:54.393
Whatever that thing you're proud of is,

327
00:11:55.390 --> 00:11:59.320
that thing required a lot of sustained focus and attention.

328
00:11:59.320 --> 00:12:02.570
And the evidence shows that when attention breaks down

329
00:12:02.570 --> 00:12:05.600
your ability to achieve your goals breaks down,

330
00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:08.410
your ability to solve your problems breaks down.

331
00:12:08.410 --> 00:12:10.230
That's why this is such a deep problem.

332
00:12:10.230 --> 00:12:14.090
When you have a long period when you can't pay attention

333
00:12:14.090 --> 00:12:15.830
I feel like it's almost like you can...

334
00:12:15.830 --> 00:12:18.360
You become almost like a stump of yourself, right?

335
00:12:18.360 --> 00:12:19.800
You can sense what you might have been

336
00:12:19.800 --> 00:12:21.520
if you've been able to apply yourself

337
00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:23.530
but you feel like you can't get there.

338
00:12:23.530 --> 00:12:25.175
This is why I think deep...

339
00:12:25.175 --> 00:12:28.400
We really need to think deeply about

340
00:12:28.400 --> 00:12:30.360
what's happened to our attention and we need to build

341
00:12:30.360 --> 00:12:32.830
the scientific solutions which I saw all over the world

342
00:12:32.830 --> 00:12:36.210
from France to New Zealand for how we get it back.

343
00:12:36.210 --> 00:12:39.310
Well, positive progress requires

344
00:12:39.310 --> 00:12:42.630
some level of sustained attention by bright people

345
00:12:42.630 --> 00:12:44.290
focused on those problems, right?

346
00:12:44.290 --> 00:12:46.550
And if there's a degradation of that,

347
00:12:46.550 --> 00:12:49.670
that doesn't bode well for the future of humankind

348
00:12:49.670 --> 00:12:50.601
and society, right?

349
00:12:50.601 --> 00:12:51.434
(Johann laughs)

350
00:12:51.434 --> 00:12:55.123
So that's why I really do think it is existential crisis.

351
00:12:56.311 --> 00:12:58.190
I think that's a really good way you just put that.

352
00:12:58.190 --> 00:13:01.520
It makes me think about this guy called Dr. James Williams,

353
00:13:01.520 --> 00:13:03.120
who worked...

354
00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:05.680
Was a senior figure at Google for many years,

355
00:13:05.680 --> 00:13:07.230
was horrified by what they were doing

356
00:13:07.230 --> 00:13:08.700
to our attention, quit.

357
00:13:08.700 --> 00:13:10.700
And his become I would argue the leading

358
00:13:10.700 --> 00:13:12.740
philosopher of attention in the world right now.

359
00:13:12.740 --> 00:13:15.100
I interviewed him in Moscow in Russia,

360
00:13:15.100 --> 00:13:17.570
and he lives there 'cause his wife works there

361
00:13:17.570 --> 00:13:19.020
for the World Health Organization.

362
00:13:19.020 --> 00:13:19.853
And he said to me...

363
00:13:19.853 --> 00:13:21.680
He gave me a metaphor that really stayed with me.

364
00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:23.220
He said, "Imagine you're driving somewhere,

365
00:13:23.220 --> 00:13:24.440
you gotta do something.

366
00:13:24.440 --> 00:13:26.800
And someone just throws an enormous bucket of mud

367
00:13:26.800 --> 00:13:28.600
over your windshield.

368
00:13:28.600 --> 00:13:29.950
Doesn't matter what you've gotta do when you get

369
00:13:29.950 --> 00:13:31.400
to your destination,

370
00:13:31.400 --> 00:13:33.950
the first thing you've gotta do is clean your windshield.

371
00:13:33.950 --> 00:13:34.783
Because you can't get any anywhere

372
00:13:34.783 --> 00:13:36.310
if you don't clean the windshield."

373
00:13:36.310 --> 00:13:38.310
And I think that's a good metaphor for the attention crisis.

374
00:13:38.310 --> 00:13:40.120
Doesn't matter what you wanna do in your life,

375
00:13:40.120 --> 00:13:42.250
if you can't pay attention you won't be able to do it.

376
00:13:42.250 --> 00:13:45.370
So the first thing we need to do is deal with the deep

377
00:13:45.370 --> 00:13:47.880
underlying factors that are damaging our attention

378
00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:50.480
in the way we eat, in the technology we use,

379
00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:53.500
in the hours we work, in the polluted air we breathe,

380
00:13:53.500 --> 00:13:56.120
there's all these factors but this to me

381
00:13:56.120 --> 00:13:58.490
is the first of the crisis.

382
00:13:58.490 --> 00:14:01.830
'Cause it's not that there aren't bigger crises in the world

383
00:14:01.830 --> 00:14:03.120
but if we don't deal with this one

384
00:14:03.120 --> 00:14:05.230
we can't get to any of the bigger crises

385
00:14:05.230 --> 00:14:08.460
either in our personal lives or in a bigger societal sense.

386
00:14:08.460 --> 00:14:11.540
Yeah, and both the problems and the solutions

387
00:14:11.540 --> 00:14:14.800
can be bifurcated into two categories,

388
00:14:14.800 --> 00:14:17.680
the systemic and the individualistic, right?

389
00:14:17.680 --> 00:14:19.130
There are certain things that we can do

390
00:14:19.130 --> 00:14:23.760
to kind of rebut this and there are also systemic problems

391
00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:24.830
that need to be addressed

392
00:14:24.830 --> 00:14:26.150
and we can talk about all of those.

393
00:14:26.150 --> 00:14:28.420
But I wanna kind of hearken back

394
00:14:28.420 --> 00:14:30.317
to the Provincetown experience

395
00:14:30.317 --> 00:14:31.150
(Johann laughs)

396
00:14:31.150 --> 00:14:31.983
because it's fresh in my mind

397
00:14:31.983 --> 00:14:35.020
because I just got back from a month sabbatical.

398
00:14:35.020 --> 00:14:36.410
I was in Hawaii.

399
00:14:36.410 --> 00:14:40.220
And unlike you I didn't leave my phone on the mainland

400
00:14:40.220 --> 00:14:43.630
but I did promise myself that I would restrict

401
00:14:43.630 --> 00:14:47.780
my access to the phone and my time spent on it.

402
00:14:47.780 --> 00:14:49.630
Like I really needed to get away from work.

403
00:14:49.630 --> 00:14:52.320
And I do this every year and it's an opportunity

404
00:14:52.320 --> 00:14:54.455
to kind of just refresh myself and relax

405
00:14:54.455 --> 00:14:59.455
and return with a renewed enthusiasm for what I do

406
00:14:59.720 --> 00:15:02.463
'cause I've tiptoed up to burnout a couple times.

407
00:15:04.160 --> 00:15:08.160
And when you're kind of endeavoring to have

408
00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:12.500
a truncated relationship with devices it becomes very...

409
00:15:12.500 --> 00:15:15.360
You're very aware and present with the allure

410
00:15:15.360 --> 00:15:16.193
and the pull of it.

411
00:15:16.193 --> 00:15:17.240
And I probably...

412
00:15:17.240 --> 00:15:18.130
I said this the other day

413
00:15:18.130 --> 00:15:21.263
but like I would give myself a B minus.

414
00:15:22.766 --> 00:15:25.980
I only checked email like once every two days.

415
00:15:25.980 --> 00:15:27.890
And I have to FaceTime with my kids

416
00:15:27.890 --> 00:15:28.800
and all that kind of stuff

417
00:15:28.800 --> 00:15:30.270
so I wasn't gonna get rid of the phone.

418
00:15:30.270 --> 00:15:33.410
But there were plenty of times where I found myself

419
00:15:33.410 --> 00:15:34.700
just scrolling for no reason.

420
00:15:34.700 --> 00:15:35.850
And I'm like, "What are you doing?

421
00:15:35.850 --> 00:15:37.310
You're here to not do that.

422
00:15:37.310 --> 00:15:39.900
You're here to have an analog experience and yet I can't."

423
00:15:39.900 --> 00:15:40.890
(chuckles)

424
00:15:40.890 --> 00:15:43.579
I would do it and there is a sense of powerlessness over it

425
00:15:43.579 --> 00:15:47.590
which goes to both the individualistic relationships

426
00:15:47.590 --> 00:15:52.590
that we have with these devices but also the great forces

427
00:15:52.930 --> 00:15:57.700
and systemic powers that are behind the scenes

428
00:15:57.700 --> 00:16:00.440
with these things that make them so irresistible.

429
00:16:00.440 --> 00:16:01.580
How did you feel different

430
00:16:01.580 --> 00:16:03.310
when you were able to go longer without it?

431
00:16:03.310 --> 00:16:04.740
Well, of course better.

432
00:16:04.740 --> 00:16:06.930
Yeah, fantastic and then...

433
00:16:06.930 --> 00:16:07.954
But then you like...

434
00:16:07.954 --> 00:16:10.537
In a moment you resort to it (laughs)

435
00:16:10.537 --> 00:16:12.370
and you're doing it again.

436
00:16:12.370 --> 00:16:14.220
And then you find yourself getting aggravated

437
00:16:14.220 --> 00:16:15.060
and then I'm like, "What are you...

438
00:16:15.060 --> 00:16:16.740
And then there's a vicious cycle

439
00:16:16.740 --> 00:16:18.803
'cause then I'm beating myself up for doing the thing

440
00:16:18.803 --> 00:16:20.900
that I said that I wasn't going to do.

441
00:16:20.900 --> 00:16:24.410
This is exactly the dilemma that I had right

442
00:16:24.410 --> 00:16:26.710
at the start of working on the book

443
00:16:26.710 --> 00:16:28.240
that led me to Provincetown

444
00:16:29.200 --> 00:16:32.530
and this period of three months completely off the internet.

445
00:16:32.530 --> 00:16:36.960
'Cause I had this moment with my godson who I call Adam

446
00:16:36.960 --> 00:16:41.870
in the book that when I realized something was really wrong.

447
00:16:41.870 --> 00:16:44.130
So when he was nine he developed this brief

448
00:16:44.130 --> 00:16:46.650
but freakishly intense obsession with Elvis

449
00:16:46.650 --> 00:16:48.320
and it was particularly cute cause he didn't know that

450
00:16:48.320 --> 00:16:49.891
Elvis had become this kind of cheesy cliche.

451
00:16:49.891 --> 00:16:53.270
So he did it with like really heart catching sincerity

452
00:16:53.270 --> 00:16:55.260
he would do this Elvis impersonation.

453
00:16:55.260 --> 00:16:57.150
And when I used to tuck him in,

454
00:16:57.150 --> 00:17:00.090
he kept getting me to tell him the story of Elvis' life

455
00:17:00.090 --> 00:17:01.430
again and again.

456
00:17:01.430 --> 00:17:02.270
I tried to skip over the bit...

457
00:17:02.270 --> 00:17:04.380
At the end where he dies on the toilet.

458
00:17:04.380 --> 00:17:06.940
And one day he said to me very intensely,

459
00:17:06.940 --> 00:17:07.947
he looked at me and said,

460
00:17:07.947 --> 00:17:10.607
"Johann, will you take me to Graceland one day?"

461
00:17:10.607 --> 00:17:13.512
And I was like, "Sure, I'll take you to Graceland one day."

462
00:17:13.512 --> 00:17:14.390
And the way you and nine year olds,

463
00:17:14.390 --> 00:17:16.447
knowing that next week it'll be Legoland or whatever.

464
00:17:16.447 --> 00:17:18.520
And he said, "No, do you really promise

465
00:17:18.520 --> 00:17:20.182
you'll take me to Graceland?"

466
00:17:20.182 --> 00:17:22.640
I was like, "I Absolutely promise."

467
00:17:22.640 --> 00:17:25.710
And I didn't think at that moment again for 10 years

468
00:17:25.710 --> 00:17:28.950
until everything had gone wrong.

469
00:17:28.950 --> 00:17:31.287
So he dropped out of school when he was 15

470
00:17:31.287 --> 00:17:36.287
and by the time he was 19 he just spent literally

471
00:17:36.980 --> 00:17:40.800
all his waking hours alternating between WhatsApp,

472
00:17:40.800 --> 00:17:43.440
Snapchat, porn, YouTube.

473
00:17:43.440 --> 00:17:46.140
And one day we were sitting on my sofa in my apartment

474
00:17:47.130 --> 00:17:48.000
and I was...

475
00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:49.500
I'd been trying to talk to him all day

476
00:17:49.500 --> 00:17:52.100
and he's a lovely, intelligent person

477
00:17:52.100 --> 00:17:54.963
and just nothing was getting any traction in his mind.

478
00:17:56.050 --> 00:17:57.700
And to be honest with you, Rich

479
00:17:57.700 --> 00:17:59.060
I wasn't that much better, right?

480
00:17:59.060 --> 00:18:01.910
I was sitting there staring at my own devices mindlessly.

481
00:18:01.910 --> 00:18:03.270
And I suddenly remembered this moment

482
00:18:03.270 --> 00:18:04.787
all these years before and I said to him,

483
00:18:04.787 --> 00:18:07.904
"Hey, let's go to Graceland."

484
00:18:07.904 --> 00:18:10.192
And he couldn't even remember this moment, right?

485
00:18:10.192 --> 00:18:11.025
He's like, "What you're talking about?"

486
00:18:11.025 --> 00:18:12.160
And I said, "No, let's go to Graceland.

487
00:18:12.160 --> 00:18:13.700
We've gotta break this numbing routine,

488
00:18:13.700 --> 00:18:14.533
let's get out of here.

489
00:18:14.533 --> 00:18:15.700
Let's go all over the south.

490
00:18:15.700 --> 00:18:17.880
But you've gotta promise me one thing

491
00:18:17.880 --> 00:18:19.760
which is that when we go you'll leave your phone

492
00:18:19.760 --> 00:18:20.850
in the hotel."

493
00:18:20.850 --> 00:18:23.100
And he said, "I absolutely promise I'll do that."

494
00:18:23.100 --> 00:18:24.840
And what if it was two weeks later we took off

495
00:18:24.840 --> 00:18:26.000
at New Orleans.

496
00:18:26.000 --> 00:18:28.950
We traveled around two weeks later we got to Graceland.

497
00:18:28.950 --> 00:18:29.940
Have you been to Graceland, Rich?

498
00:18:29.940 --> 00:18:30.773
No.

499
00:18:30.773 --> 00:18:31.825
Oh, I'm gonna take you one day.

500
00:18:31.825 --> 00:18:33.645
So when you arrive at the gates-

501
00:18:33.645 --> 00:18:34.760
(indistinct) is that a promise?

502
00:18:34.760 --> 00:18:35.972
I promise 10 years from now-

503
00:18:35.972 --> 00:18:36.934
(Johann and Rich laughing)

504
00:18:36.934 --> 00:18:37.767
Yeah, 10 years.

505
00:18:37.767 --> 00:18:38.600
There gonna be some broken (indistinct).

506
00:18:38.600 --> 00:18:39.780
But I'm a 65 year old man.

507
00:18:39.780 --> 00:18:40.678
Yeah, you gonna take me to Graceland.

508
00:18:40.678 --> 00:18:41.790
(Johann laughs)

509
00:18:41.790 --> 00:18:42.913
We'll do it.

510
00:18:42.913 --> 00:18:45.220
And it's a funny thing cause even before COVID,

511
00:18:45.220 --> 00:18:48.770
when you get to Graceland now there's no person

512
00:18:48.770 --> 00:18:49.603
to show you around.

513
00:18:49.603 --> 00:18:50.910
What happened is they hand you an iPad

514
00:18:50.910 --> 00:18:53.410
and you put in earbuds and the iPad shows you around.

515
00:18:53.410 --> 00:18:55.513
So it says, go left, go right, whatever.

516
00:18:56.360 --> 00:18:59.930
And as you're walking around there's a representation

517
00:18:59.930 --> 00:19:02.120
of the room that you're in on the iPad, right?

518
00:19:02.120 --> 00:19:04.420
And it erase the history of it whatever.

519
00:19:04.420 --> 00:19:06.890
So what happens is everyone just walks around Graceland

520
00:19:06.890 --> 00:19:09.700
staring at their iPad or barely looking up, right?

521
00:19:09.700 --> 00:19:12.030
And I'm walking around I'm feeling like more and more tense,

522
00:19:12.030 --> 00:19:14.310
like trying to make eye contact to people who looking down.

523
00:19:14.310 --> 00:19:16.180
And we got to the Jungle Room,

524
00:19:16.180 --> 00:19:18.453
which was Elvis' favorite room in Graceland.

525
00:19:19.430 --> 00:19:22.100
And there was a Canadian couple next to us

526
00:19:22.100 --> 00:19:24.857
and the husband turned to his wife and said,

527
00:19:24.857 --> 00:19:25.950
"Honey, this is amazing.

528
00:19:25.950 --> 00:19:29.540
Look, if you swipe left, you can see the Jungle Room

529
00:19:29.540 --> 00:19:31.730
to the left and if you swipe right,

530
00:19:31.730 --> 00:19:34.000
you can see the Jungle Room to the right.

531
00:19:34.000 --> 00:19:35.538
And I laugh 'cause I thought he was kidding.

532
00:19:35.538 --> 00:19:37.350
And I turn to looked at him and they're just swiping

533
00:19:37.350 --> 00:19:39.887
back and forth and I leaned over and I said,

534
00:19:39.887 --> 00:19:42.620
"But hey sir, there's an old fashion inform of swiping

535
00:19:42.620 --> 00:19:45.300
you could do it's called turning your head

536
00:19:45.300 --> 00:19:48.260
'cause look we're actually in the Jungle Room, right?

537
00:19:48.260 --> 00:19:50.010
You don't have to look at it on your screen.

538
00:19:50.010 --> 00:19:51.480
We're actually there."

539
00:19:51.480 --> 00:19:53.860
And they backed away clearly thinking I was insane.

540
00:19:53.860 --> 00:19:57.310
And I turned to my godson to laugh about it

541
00:19:57.310 --> 00:19:58.910
and he was in the corner of the room just flicking

542
00:19:58.910 --> 00:20:00.980
through Snapchat 'cause he could not stop

543
00:20:00.980 --> 00:20:03.530
from the minute we land he couldn't stop.

544
00:20:03.530 --> 00:20:04.987
And I went up to him and I just said,

545
00:20:04.987 --> 00:20:07.730
"Look, I know you are afraid of missing out

546
00:20:07.730 --> 00:20:09.860
but this is guaranteeing that you are miss out.

547
00:20:09.860 --> 00:20:11.600
You're not showing up to your own life.

548
00:20:11.600 --> 00:20:14.250
You're not pre isn't your own existence, right?

549
00:20:14.250 --> 00:20:15.970
This is no way to live."

550
00:20:15.970 --> 00:20:19.050
And he stormed off and I wondered around Memphis

551
00:20:19.050 --> 00:20:19.883
on my own that day.

552
00:20:19.883 --> 00:20:22.210
And I found him that night in the Heartbreak Hotel

553
00:20:22.210 --> 00:20:24.150
where we were staying across the street

554
00:20:25.000 --> 00:20:28.120
and he was sitting by the guitar (indistinct) swimming pool.

555
00:20:28.120 --> 00:20:30.720
And I went out to him and I apologized for getting angry

556
00:20:30.720 --> 00:20:33.640
and he was just scrolling and scrolling and didn't look up

557
00:20:33.640 --> 00:20:36.370
but he said, "I know something's really wrong

558
00:20:36.370 --> 00:20:37.850
but I dunno what it is."

559
00:20:37.850 --> 00:20:38.810
And that's when I thought

560
00:20:38.810 --> 00:20:39.977
okay, I need to investigate this.

561
00:20:39.977 --> 00:20:42.053
And when I came back from Memphis,

562
00:20:42.980 --> 00:20:47.280
I was just so tired of being wired.

563
00:20:47.280 --> 00:20:50.460
I was disgusted by what I saw happening to me,

564
00:20:50.460 --> 00:20:52.250
to so many people around me.

565
00:20:52.250 --> 00:20:55.090
And at that time I still was stuck in those two stories

566
00:20:55.090 --> 00:20:56.840
that this was a failure of willpower

567
00:20:56.840 --> 00:20:58.080
and someone invented the smartphone.

568
00:20:58.080 --> 00:21:00.680
I later learned there's a much more interesting

569
00:21:00.680 --> 00:21:03.000
and complex story happening to all of us.

570
00:21:03.000 --> 00:21:04.940
So I thought okay those are the two problems

571
00:21:04.940 --> 00:21:06.080
I've got a solution here.

572
00:21:06.080 --> 00:21:07.950
I was lucky the film writes to one of my books

573
00:21:07.950 --> 00:21:09.370
had just been sold.

574
00:21:09.370 --> 00:21:10.230
It was made into a film called

575
00:21:10.230 --> 00:21:12.260
The United States Vs. Billie Holiday

576
00:21:12.260 --> 00:21:16.550
And I just thought I'm done,

577
00:21:16.550 --> 00:21:18.490
I'm gonna take this money and I'm gonna go away

578
00:21:18.490 --> 00:21:21.960
for three months and I'm gonna be completely offline.

579
00:21:21.960 --> 00:21:24.520
So I rented a little sliver of a beach house

580
00:21:24.520 --> 00:21:26.678
in Provincetown in Cape Cod.

581
00:21:26.678 --> 00:21:31.360
And I left my phone and my laptop in Boston, right?

582
00:21:31.360 --> 00:21:33.910
My smartphone and I took a laptop...

583
00:21:33.910 --> 00:21:35.283
My friend (indistinct) gave me his broken old laptop

584
00:21:35.283 --> 00:21:37.640
that can't get online.

585
00:21:37.640 --> 00:21:39.580
And yeah and those three months taught me

586
00:21:39.580 --> 00:21:41.430
just a huge amount.

587
00:21:41.430 --> 00:21:43.870
Right, so during that period of time

588
00:21:43.870 --> 00:21:46.410
I'm sure you didn't really talk about this in the book

589
00:21:46.410 --> 00:21:48.150
but I would suspect for at least...

590
00:21:48.150 --> 00:21:50.080
I don't know, seven to 10 days there had to be

591
00:21:50.080 --> 00:21:53.240
kind of a detoxification like weaning off

592
00:21:53.240 --> 00:21:55.282
like kind of you're grab...

593
00:21:55.282 --> 00:21:57.360
Grabbing for the phone that's not there

594
00:21:57.360 --> 00:22:00.540
or just feeling antsy because you can't get that

595
00:22:00.540 --> 00:22:05.230
immediate dopamine fixed that the phone will always provide?

596
00:22:05.230 --> 00:22:09.760
It is funny that was the first five or six days

597
00:22:09.760 --> 00:22:11.510
were just like a haze of decompression

598
00:22:11.510 --> 00:22:13.310
I almost felt stoned, right?

599
00:22:13.310 --> 00:22:15.230
Like just...

600
00:22:15.230 --> 00:22:18.020
I felt a tremendous likeness and sense of relief

601
00:22:18.020 --> 00:22:18.980
but I was exhausted by that.

602
00:22:18.980 --> 00:22:22.126
I had been working unbelievably hard in the run up to it

603
00:22:22.126 --> 00:22:25.090
that might have just been just being on a break, right?

604
00:22:25.090 --> 00:22:26.450
And then but I kept having these...

605
00:22:26.450 --> 00:22:29.780
It was a physical response where I would...

606
00:22:29.780 --> 00:22:31.629
Exactly what you describe I pat my pocket I'm feeling panic

607
00:22:31.629 --> 00:22:33.060
you think shit where am I put my phone?

608
00:22:33.060 --> 00:22:35.193
You're like, oh yeah, you don't have it.

609
00:22:35.193 --> 00:22:36.026
I had a...

610
00:22:36.026 --> 00:22:38.130
I'd bought a phone it's called the Jitterbug.

611
00:22:38.130 --> 00:22:39.410
It's the only phone you can buy

612
00:22:39.410 --> 00:22:40.780
in the United States now that won't...

613
00:22:40.780 --> 00:22:42.149
It has no internet capacity.

614
00:22:42.149 --> 00:22:42.982
(Rich laughs)

615
00:22:42.982 --> 00:22:45.150
It's designed for extremely old people as enormous buttons.

616
00:22:45.150 --> 00:22:46.630
And it has an emergency button

617
00:22:46.630 --> 00:22:47.788
so that if you fall over

618
00:22:47.788 --> 00:22:48.621
it will call the nearest hospital.

619
00:22:48.621 --> 00:22:50.940
So I thought all right, it's win-win.

620
00:22:50.940 --> 00:22:54.950
And it's funny I would open that phone in those seven days

621
00:22:54.950 --> 00:22:57.018
and just sort of stab at the buttons.

622
00:22:57.018 --> 00:22:57.957
Couldn't do any...

623
00:22:57.957 --> 00:22:59.097
The phone literally does nothing.

624
00:22:59.097 --> 00:23:01.360
And it reminded me of when I was a kid I saw maybe

625
00:23:01.360 --> 00:23:04.200
some David Attenborough wildlife documentary

626
00:23:04.200 --> 00:23:06.900
of a mother penguin whose baby has died.

627
00:23:06.900 --> 00:23:08.760
And she just keeps nudging it to try to get it

628
00:23:08.760 --> 00:23:09.774
to do something.

629
00:23:09.774 --> 00:23:14.774
And I kept the of that and then I had a real crash,

630
00:23:16.070 --> 00:23:17.231
a terrible crash.

631
00:23:17.231 --> 00:23:18.470
It was interesting.

632
00:23:18.470 --> 00:23:23.470
I remember I was walking down the beach in Provincetown

633
00:23:23.700 --> 00:23:27.450
behind the West End of Provincetown for people who know it.

634
00:23:27.450 --> 00:23:29.270
And I was seeing what I'd seen in Memphis, right?

635
00:23:29.270 --> 00:23:30.290
People just...

636
00:23:30.290 --> 00:23:31.730
Provincetown is one of the most beautiful places

637
00:23:31.730 --> 00:23:32.700
in the world.

638
00:23:32.700 --> 00:23:34.290
And people were just looking at their phones.

639
00:23:34.290 --> 00:23:35.327
People with kids weren't even looking at their kids

640
00:23:35.327 --> 00:23:36.980
it was driving me insane.

641
00:23:36.980 --> 00:23:37.813
It would dri...

642
00:23:37.813 --> 00:23:39.040
Normally it would drive me insane

643
00:23:39.040 --> 00:23:40.630
but this time instead of saying to them,

644
00:23:40.630 --> 00:23:42.210
oh, you're not present in your lives.

645
00:23:42.210 --> 00:23:43.480
You're not...

646
00:23:43.480 --> 00:23:45.210
I wanted to go up to them and go gimme that phone.

647
00:23:45.210 --> 00:23:46.076
I want it, right?

648
00:23:46.076 --> 00:23:46.909
(chuckles)

649
00:23:46.909 --> 00:23:48.000
I wanted to...

650
00:23:48.000 --> 00:23:51.320
And it was interesting this tremendous hunger

651
00:23:52.230 --> 00:23:55.690
'cause I realized for 15 years prior to that

652
00:23:55.690 --> 00:23:58.370
throughout the day I had been completely climatized

653
00:23:58.370 --> 00:24:01.350
to receiving the kind of thin insistent signals

654
00:24:01.350 --> 00:24:03.460
we get from the web which are designed as rewards

655
00:24:03.460 --> 00:24:04.853
to keep us coming back.

656
00:24:04.853 --> 00:24:09.029
Retweets, hearts, likes, we all know how they work.

657
00:24:09.029 --> 00:24:11.164
And when they were gone...

658
00:24:11.164 --> 00:24:14.360
This will bit sound a bit pretentious,

659
00:24:14.360 --> 00:24:15.800
it is a bit pretentious.

660
00:24:15.800 --> 00:24:16.880
But Simone de Beauvoir,

661
00:24:16.880 --> 00:24:18.760
the great French philosopher said that

662
00:24:18.760 --> 00:24:19.860
when she became an atheist,

663
00:24:19.860 --> 00:24:22.320
it was like the world had gone silent.

664
00:24:22.320 --> 00:24:23.210
And that's how I felt.

665
00:24:23.210 --> 00:24:25.690
I felt like the world had gone silent, right?

666
00:24:25.690 --> 00:24:29.340
Like these signals I was so used to were gone

667
00:24:30.195 --> 00:24:32.430
and no normal social interaction

668
00:24:32.430 --> 00:24:33.710
even though I was meeting amazing people

669
00:24:33.710 --> 00:24:35.610
they don't flood you with hearts the minute you meet them

670
00:24:35.610 --> 00:24:37.647
that would be a very weird social interaction, right?

671
00:24:37.647 --> 00:24:42.493
And that was when I realized a big part of this,

672
00:24:43.460 --> 00:24:46.630
if you just extricate yourself in that that a vacuum,

673
00:24:46.630 --> 00:24:48.480
you need to then fill that vacuum.

674
00:24:48.480 --> 00:24:50.090
And that was when I started to read a lot more

675
00:24:50.090 --> 00:24:52.120
and later interview the leading scientist

676
00:24:52.120 --> 00:24:53.887
in the world on flow states.

677
00:24:53.887 --> 00:24:55.387
And that was how I kind of got out of it

678
00:24:55.387 --> 00:24:57.590
we can talk about that a bit if you like.

679
00:24:57.590 --> 00:24:59.410
Yeah, I mean, there's a lot to unpack there

680
00:24:59.410 --> 00:25:04.410
but I think kind of the lesson that I think is powerful

681
00:25:07.780 --> 00:25:09.840
for anybody who's listening to this is

682
00:25:09.840 --> 00:25:12.530
over that course of three months through

683
00:25:12.530 --> 00:25:15.230
that detachment process you were able

684
00:25:15.230 --> 00:25:18.310
to kind of restore your sleep

685
00:25:18.310 --> 00:25:20.300
like sleeping better than ever,

686
00:25:20.300 --> 00:25:23.530
repair your ability to sit down and work on a book

687
00:25:23.530 --> 00:25:27.440
and focus, be tuned into the news by reading

688
00:25:27.440 --> 00:25:29.405
a newspaper every day and then that being that

689
00:25:29.405 --> 00:25:30.757
for the rest of the day.

690
00:25:30.757 --> 00:25:31.590
(Johann chuckles)

691
00:25:31.590 --> 00:25:34.270
Connect with friends, be present in your life.

692
00:25:34.270 --> 00:25:38.300
And you explain this time of waking up and not feeling

693
00:25:38.300 --> 00:25:42.060
like you needed to drink coffee and feeling just awake

694
00:25:42.060 --> 00:25:44.900
in a way that you hadn't since you were a young boy.

695
00:25:44.900 --> 00:25:48.210
And a lot of this is about returning

696
00:25:48.210 --> 00:25:50.930
to a more natural state of man, right?

697
00:25:50.930 --> 00:25:53.750
We live in the modern world we're not gonna go live in caves

698
00:25:53.750 --> 00:25:56.140
or anything like that and it's unrealistic

699
00:25:56.140 --> 00:25:58.530
that we're gonna be luddites.

700
00:25:58.530 --> 00:26:01.410
But to the extent that we can exert some control

701
00:26:01.410 --> 00:26:04.970
over these tectonic forces that are driving us

702
00:26:04.970 --> 00:26:08.040
to this exhausted state there are things that we can do.

703
00:26:08.040 --> 00:26:12.462
And your experience as an example of how

704
00:26:12.462 --> 00:26:17.462
these natural states that have alluded us can be restored.

705
00:26:18.500 --> 00:26:19.800
Yeah, yes, so you put it really well.

706
00:26:19.800 --> 00:26:22.237
It was one of the things that amazed me...

707
00:26:22.237 --> 00:26:24.520
'Cause I thought I was nearly 40 at the time.

708
00:26:24.520 --> 00:26:25.400
I thought oh, maybe what's happening

709
00:26:25.400 --> 00:26:26.917
is I've just got older, right?

710
00:26:26.917 --> 00:26:29.460
And maybe a brain just gets deters.

711
00:26:29.460 --> 00:26:31.160
My attention went back to being as good as it had been

712
00:26:31.160 --> 00:26:32.173
when I was 17.

713
00:26:33.790 --> 00:26:35.570
I could sit and read a book for eight hours a day

714
00:26:35.570 --> 00:26:37.200
and focus deeply.

715
00:26:37.200 --> 00:26:39.060
And I later realized that wasn't just about

716
00:26:39.060 --> 00:26:41.670
the tech withdrawal that was many things,

717
00:26:41.670 --> 00:26:42.700
many changes that happened.

718
00:26:42.700 --> 00:26:44.890
I completely changed the way I ate, these...

719
00:26:44.890 --> 00:26:46.280
None of these things were conscious at the time

720
00:26:46.280 --> 00:26:48.160
it was when I later interviewed the scientists

721
00:26:48.160 --> 00:26:49.340
who've researched this sort of thing

722
00:26:49.340 --> 00:26:52.293
that I realized how much those changes have weighed on me.

723
00:26:52.293 --> 00:26:54.940
My sleep, he mentioned lots of factors.

724
00:26:54.940 --> 00:26:59.037
But I think you're right there's two ways

725
00:27:00.940 --> 00:27:04.040
we have to respond to this crisis or two kinds of way

726
00:27:04.040 --> 00:27:06.240
we've gotta respond to this crisis.

727
00:27:06.240 --> 00:27:08.980
I think of them as defense and offense, right?

728
00:27:08.980 --> 00:27:10.800
We've gotta defend ourselves and our children

729
00:27:10.800 --> 00:27:12.610
as much as possible from these forces

730
00:27:12.610 --> 00:27:14.960
that are pouring acid on our attention.

731
00:27:14.960 --> 00:27:15.890
And I talk a lot...

732
00:27:15.890 --> 00:27:19.410
Give dozens of examples in the book about things we can do.

733
00:27:19.410 --> 00:27:22.100
But I wanna be honest with people about this

734
00:27:22.100 --> 00:27:23.270
that will hugely help.

735
00:27:23.270 --> 00:27:25.030
I'm passionately in favor of those things.

736
00:27:25.030 --> 00:27:27.480
I try to do all of them myself

737
00:27:27.480 --> 00:27:29.260
but that will only get you so far.

738
00:27:29.260 --> 00:27:30.560
'Cause at the moment it's like someone

739
00:27:30.560 --> 00:27:33.250
is pouring itching powder over us all the time

740
00:27:33.250 --> 00:27:34.390
and then leaning forward and going,

741
00:27:34.390 --> 00:27:36.560
hey buddy, you might wanna learn how to meditate

742
00:27:36.560 --> 00:27:37.780
then you wouldn't scratch so much.

743
00:27:37.780 --> 00:27:39.000
And you wanna apply going well,

744
00:27:39.000 --> 00:27:40.151
screw you I'll learn to meditate.

745
00:27:40.151 --> 00:27:40.984
(Rich chuckles)

746
00:27:40.984 --> 00:27:42.210
You stop pouring itching powder on me.

747
00:27:42.210 --> 00:27:44.580
Which is why we've gotta have an element of offense,

748
00:27:44.580 --> 00:27:46.790
we've gotta take on the forces that are doing this to us.

749
00:27:46.790 --> 00:27:48.940
Now that can sound very fancy and grand.

750
00:27:48.940 --> 00:27:51.630
I talk about lots of practical ways that can be done

751
00:27:51.630 --> 00:27:54.120
and ways it has already been done in many parts of the world

752
00:27:54.120 --> 00:27:56.720
and ways it was done in the past in the United States.

753
00:27:56.720 --> 00:27:59.157
But you you're right that we've gotta get...

754
00:27:59.157 --> 00:28:02.270
When you say this about the way we live

755
00:28:02.270 --> 00:28:03.800
you're absolutely right.

756
00:28:03.800 --> 00:28:05.210
Even I was doing that I thought

757
00:28:05.210 --> 00:28:06.460
well, this is not sustainable.

758
00:28:06.460 --> 00:28:07.590
And I remember...

759
00:28:08.460 --> 00:28:09.680
So of course, what I was asking was

760
00:28:09.680 --> 00:28:11.490
well, how can we integrate some of these insights

761
00:28:11.490 --> 00:28:12.930
into our normal lives?

762
00:28:12.930 --> 00:28:15.730
And I remember the last day I was in Provincetown

763
00:28:15.730 --> 00:28:17.280
going to what's it called?

764
00:28:17.280 --> 00:28:20.290
Where the lighthouse is there's a spot where you can

765
00:28:20.290 --> 00:28:21.595
look back over the whole of Provincetown.

766
00:28:21.595 --> 00:28:23.740
I hadn't left Provincetown the whole three months.

767
00:28:23.740 --> 00:28:26.520
I hadn't been in a moving vehicle for three months.

768
00:28:26.520 --> 00:28:28.610
So I could see the kind of whole landscape

769
00:28:28.610 --> 00:28:31.460
of where I had been (clears throat)

770
00:28:31.460 --> 00:28:34.070
and remember thinking,

771
00:28:34.070 --> 00:28:36.750
well, I'm never gonna go back to living how I lived before.

772
00:28:36.750 --> 00:28:37.910
Why would I do that?

773
00:28:37.910 --> 00:28:39.450
This has been amazing, right?

774
00:28:39.450 --> 00:28:44.450
The joys of deep thought of reflection, of mind wandering,

775
00:28:44.920 --> 00:28:47.280
of the joys of being present in your life,

776
00:28:47.280 --> 00:28:49.310
they're so profound.

777
00:28:49.310 --> 00:28:51.333
Why would I go back?

778
00:28:51.333 --> 00:28:52.620
And I got the boat back to Boston,

779
00:28:52.620 --> 00:28:54.590
I got the ferry back to Boston.

780
00:28:54.590 --> 00:28:56.820
I got horribly sick on the journey.

781
00:28:56.820 --> 00:29:01.240
My friend (indistinct) gave me back my laptop and my phone.

782
00:29:01.240 --> 00:29:04.410
And within a month I'd say I was 80% back where I've been.

783
00:29:04.410 --> 00:29:05.472
Yeah, that was the...

784
00:29:05.472 --> 00:29:06.305
