before that seemingly innocuous interruption.

1215
00:45:30.770 --> 00:45:32.217
Yeah, it's so important, Rich.

1216
00:45:32.217 --> 00:45:33.330
And what you're saying is so right.

1217
00:45:33.330 --> 00:45:35.530
And for people who dunno about flow states,

1218
00:45:35.530 --> 00:45:37.410
everyone listening will have experienced a flow state

1219
00:45:37.410 --> 00:45:39.090
in your life, in their lives.

1220
00:45:39.090 --> 00:45:42.190
So a flow state is when you're doing something

1221
00:45:42.190 --> 00:45:43.920
and you really get into it.

1222
00:45:43.920 --> 00:45:47.140
You get into the zone and your sense of time falls away,

1223
00:45:47.140 --> 00:45:49.410
your sense of ego falls away and you are just in it.

1224
00:45:49.410 --> 00:45:51.677
The way one rock climber put it is,

1225
00:45:51.677 --> 00:45:52.680
"When you're in flow,

1226
00:45:52.680 --> 00:45:55.141
you feel like you are the rock you're climbing."

1227
00:45:55.141 --> 00:45:57.470
And flow is really important for the attention debate

1228
00:45:57.470 --> 00:45:59.900
'cause flow is simultaneously the deepest

1229
00:45:59.900 --> 00:46:01.090
form of attention we can provide.

1230
00:46:01.090 --> 00:46:05.270
When you're in a flow state your attention is fully on it

1231
00:46:05.270 --> 00:46:06.800
and once you get into it,

1232
00:46:06.800 --> 00:46:09.410
it's the easiest form of attention to provide, right?

1233
00:46:09.410 --> 00:46:12.090
When you're in a flow state it's not like memorizing facts

1234
00:46:12.090 --> 00:46:12.923
for an exam.

1235
00:46:12.923 --> 00:46:13.920
It's like, oh, okay what is that?

1236
00:46:13.920 --> 00:46:14.812
What's that?

1237
00:46:14.812 --> 00:46:18.570
It's like a gusher of attention inside all of us.

1238
00:46:18.570 --> 00:46:20.300
So obviously, one thing I wanted to understand is

1239
00:46:20.300 --> 00:46:23.010
okay, if it's a gusher of attention inside all of us

1240
00:46:23.010 --> 00:46:23.890
where do we drill?

1241
00:46:23.890 --> 00:46:24.900
How do we get it?

1242
00:46:24.900 --> 00:46:27.620
So I went to interview Professor Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi,

1243
00:46:27.620 --> 00:46:29.050
you have no idea how long it took me to learn how

1244
00:46:29.050 --> 00:46:30.100
to say that.

1245
00:46:30.100 --> 00:46:32.260
Who was the man who coined the phrase

1246
00:46:32.260 --> 00:46:33.680
flow states in the 1960s.

1247
00:46:33.680 --> 00:46:35.060
A completely...

1248
00:46:35.060 --> 00:46:36.470
One of the most important psychologists

1249
00:46:36.470 --> 00:46:38.380
of the last half century I would argue.

1250
00:46:38.380 --> 00:46:40.990
And he spent 50 years studying flow states.

1251
00:46:40.990 --> 00:46:44.660
Sadly, I think I did the last interview he ever did

1252
00:46:44.660 --> 00:46:47.050
'cause he died shortly afterwards.

1253
00:46:47.050 --> 00:46:50.680
And Professor Csikszentmihalyi said to me

1254
00:46:50.680 --> 00:46:52.250
many important things about flow.

1255
00:46:52.250 --> 00:46:54.440
But for me I think there are three be really useful

1256
00:46:54.440 --> 00:46:55.780
for your listeners and your viewers

1257
00:46:55.780 --> 00:46:58.710
if they wanna maximize their chances of getting into flow.

1258
00:46:58.710 --> 00:47:00.460
So there's three things you can do.

1259
00:47:00.460 --> 00:47:02.210
I mean, there's lots but I would say these are the three

1260
00:47:02.210 --> 00:47:03.610
most important.

1261
00:47:03.610 --> 00:47:08.610
The first is you've got to choose one goal and filter out

1262
00:47:09.690 --> 00:47:11.820
everything else you're trying to do, right?

1263
00:47:11.820 --> 00:47:13.560
If you're trying to do more than one thing at a time

1264
00:47:13.560 --> 00:47:14.600
you won't get into to flow.

1265
00:47:14.600 --> 00:47:16.930
So you've got to narrow your focus.

1266
00:47:16.930 --> 00:47:18.420
I wanna paint this canvas.

1267
00:47:18.420 --> 00:47:19.600
I wanna climb this rock.

1268
00:47:19.600 --> 00:47:21.030
I wanna write this chapter.

1269
00:47:21.030 --> 00:47:22.940
You gotta do one thing.

1270
00:47:22.940 --> 00:47:24.970
The second thing is the goal you choose

1271
00:47:24.970 --> 00:47:27.970
has got to be meaningful to you, right?

1272
00:47:27.970 --> 00:47:30.540
So for you it might be painting a canvas,

1273
00:47:30.540 --> 00:47:32.863
for me I can't paint for shit, right?

1274
00:47:32.863 --> 00:47:34.200
I would normally get to flow on that.

1275
00:47:34.200 --> 00:47:35.880
For you it might be going for a run.

1276
00:47:35.880 --> 00:47:37.710
The only time I ever went for a run is when I once thought

1277
00:47:37.710 --> 00:47:38.927
KFC was gonna close and I got there

1278
00:47:38.927 --> 00:47:40.780
and it was another hour to go.

1279
00:47:40.780 --> 00:47:41.920
Waste of time that was.

1280
00:47:41.920 --> 00:47:44.713
So and yeah so different people will have different things

1281
00:47:44.713 --> 00:47:45.950
that get them into flow.

1282
00:47:45.950 --> 00:47:48.050
If you're trying to focus on something

1283
00:47:48.050 --> 00:47:50.010
that is not meaningful to you,

1284
00:47:50.010 --> 00:47:52.360
your attention will just slip and slide off it.

1285
00:47:52.360 --> 00:47:57.313
And the third is it will really help if you choose something

1286
00:47:59.983 --> 00:48:02.720
that is at the edge of your comfort zone,

1287
00:48:02.720 --> 00:48:03.930
at the edge of your ability.

1288
00:48:03.930 --> 00:48:06.640
So let's say you're a middle talent rock climber, right?

1289
00:48:06.640 --> 00:48:08.420
You don't wanna just climb over your garden wall.

1290
00:48:08.420 --> 00:48:09.831
That's not gonna get you into flow it's too easy.

1291
00:48:09.831 --> 00:48:12.140
Equally you don't wanna suddenly tomorrow

1292
00:48:12.140 --> 00:48:14.770
climb Mount Kilimanjaro it's gonna be too overwhelming.

1293
00:48:14.770 --> 00:48:18.500
You wanna choose a slightly higher and harder rock face

1294
00:48:18.500 --> 00:48:19.720
than the one you climbed last time.

1295
00:48:19.720 --> 00:48:21.050
So if you do these three things,

1296
00:48:21.050 --> 00:48:24.680
narrow down to one goal, make sure it's a meaningful goal,

1297
00:48:24.680 --> 00:48:26.590
choose one at the edge of your comfort zone,

1298
00:48:26.590 --> 00:48:29.340
you'll maximize your chances of getting that deep

1299
00:48:29.340 --> 00:48:30.410
gusher of attention.

1300
00:48:30.410 --> 00:48:31.950
But even as I say that, Rich,

1301
00:48:31.950 --> 00:48:34.600
you can see like you're saying how the environment

1302
00:48:34.600 --> 00:48:36.700
in which we live is militating.

1303
00:48:36.700 --> 00:48:38.167
Step one, killing it, right?

1304
00:48:38.167 --> 00:48:40.040
You've gotta do one thing.

1305
00:48:40.040 --> 00:48:41.470
If you're being constantly texting...

1306
00:48:41.470 --> 00:48:43.050
There was a study by Professor Michael Posner,

1307
00:48:43.050 --> 00:48:44.510
at the University of Oregon.

1308
00:48:44.510 --> 00:48:47.640
That found if you are interrupted it takes you on average

1309
00:48:47.640 --> 00:48:50.600
23 minutes to get back to the level of focus you had

1310
00:48:50.600 --> 00:48:52.030
before you were interrupted.

1311
00:48:52.030 --> 00:48:55.070
But many of us are never getting 23 minutes to spare, right?

1312
00:48:55.070 --> 00:48:56.980
You think about something as simple as

1313
00:48:56.980 --> 00:48:58.580
someone texts you and they're off

1314
00:48:58.580 --> 00:48:59.760
you didn't immediately reply

1315
00:48:59.760 --> 00:49:02.610
and they go, "But it would've only taken you two seconds?"

1316
00:49:02.610 --> 00:49:04.067
And one of the things I've been trying to educate people

1317
00:49:04.067 --> 00:49:06.337
in my life is saying,

1318
00:49:06.337 --> 00:49:08.310
"You think it will only take two seconds,

1319
00:49:08.310 --> 00:49:11.910
actually it'll take me two seconds plus the 23 minutes

1320
00:49:11.910 --> 00:49:14.481
it takes me to get back to the focus I had before."

1321
00:49:14.481 --> 00:49:18.010
If you and I were checking our texts now I'd be going...

1322
00:49:18.010 --> 00:49:19.610
While you were saying the really interesting things

1323
00:49:19.610 --> 00:49:20.443
you just said.

1324
00:49:20.443 --> 00:49:21.276
If I just glance at my text

1325
00:49:21.276 --> 00:49:23.560
it feels like such a small thing.

1326
00:49:23.560 --> 00:49:24.960
And then I'd have to focus on text

1327
00:49:24.960 --> 00:49:26.440
oh, my friend texted me and said that.

1328
00:49:26.440 --> 00:49:27.640
But what's Rich saying?

1329
00:49:27.640 --> 00:49:31.030
And that incurs the switch cost effect and it kills flow.

1330
00:49:31.030 --> 00:49:32.216
So this is, yeah.

1331
00:49:32.216 --> 00:49:34.600
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

1332
00:49:34.600 --> 00:49:38.150
Which means you have to exercise a lot of diligence,

1333
00:49:38.150 --> 00:49:39.854
or to put it in the cases.

1334
00:49:39.854 --> 00:49:41.771
The way I do it is I sort of...

1335
00:49:41.771 --> 00:49:44.070
I'm like all right, I'm gonna work on this

1336
00:49:44.070 --> 00:49:45.220
for a certain period of time.

1337
00:49:45.220 --> 00:49:48.660
And then maybe I'll reward myself with

1338
00:49:48.660 --> 00:49:51.970
five minutes of checking messages to make sure the world

1339
00:49:51.970 --> 00:49:53.692
didn't explode in the meantime.

1340
00:49:53.692 --> 00:49:54.525
(Johann laughs)

1341
00:49:54.525 --> 00:49:56.780
But going into that understanding that even if that takes

1342
00:49:56.780 --> 00:49:59.080
five minutes it's not five minutes,

1343
00:49:59.080 --> 00:50:02.570
it's like 35 minutes of distracted time.

1344
00:50:02.570 --> 00:50:04.530
And am I willing to budget for that?

1345
00:50:04.530 --> 00:50:08.840
Or is that cause too severe and at odds with the goal

1346
00:50:08.840 --> 00:50:10.820
that I'm trying to achieve?

1347
00:50:10.820 --> 00:50:12.870
I think that's such a good way of putting it and you...

1348
00:50:12.870 --> 00:50:15.700
What's happening is a cumulative effect, right?

1349
00:50:15.700 --> 00:50:18.670
Where our attention is being degraded and degraded,

1350
00:50:18.670 --> 00:50:19.813
and degraded.

1351
00:50:20.720 --> 00:50:21.800
And I think it's worth thinking about

1352
00:50:21.800 --> 00:50:24.140
some of the other factors that people can do

1353
00:50:24.140 --> 00:50:24.973
in their own lives.

1354
00:50:24.973 --> 00:50:27.320
I think it will be really relevant to a lot of your views.

1355
00:50:27.320 --> 00:50:29.580
I mean, there's a big load of things we can do

1356
00:50:29.580 --> 00:50:32.360
about childhood now straight away.

1357
00:50:32.360 --> 00:50:33.193
I'd like to talk about them,

1358
00:50:33.193 --> 00:50:36.140
but let's look at food for a second, right?

1359
00:50:36.140 --> 00:50:37.710
So there's this really interesting movement

1360
00:50:37.710 --> 00:50:40.970
I know you know about called nutritional psychiatry.

1361
00:50:40.970 --> 00:50:44.320
A psychiatrists who are looking at how the way we eat

1362
00:50:44.320 --> 00:50:48.080
profoundly affects our brains function.

1363
00:50:48.080 --> 00:50:49.570
And there's three...

1364
00:50:49.570 --> 00:50:51.360
I interviewed loads of these nutritional psychiatrists

1365
00:50:51.360 --> 00:50:53.130
and other people who are experts on this.

1366
00:50:53.130 --> 00:50:55.320
And there's kind of three big ways...

1367
00:50:55.320 --> 00:50:56.500
There's actually more but there's three

1368
00:50:56.500 --> 00:50:58.390
in particularly big ways.

1369
00:50:58.390 --> 00:51:00.430
In which the way we eat at the moment

1370
00:51:00.430 --> 00:51:02.900
is damaging our ability specifically to focus

1371
00:51:02.900 --> 00:51:04.330
and pay attention.

1372
00:51:04.330 --> 00:51:06.590
So the first is very simple.

1373
00:51:06.590 --> 00:51:08.700
So let's imagine you have the standard

1374
00:51:08.700 --> 00:51:10.910
British or American breakfast that you grow up...

1375
00:51:10.910 --> 00:51:12.850
Most people in our countries have.

1376
00:51:12.850 --> 00:51:16.930
You eat in the morning sugary cereal and white bread,

1377
00:51:16.930 --> 00:51:18.440
or white bread with butter on it or whatever,

1378
00:51:18.440 --> 00:51:19.770
stuff like that.

1379
00:51:19.770 --> 00:51:24.060
What that does is that releases a huge amount of energy

1380
00:51:24.060 --> 00:51:25.840
really quickly into your brain.

1381
00:51:25.840 --> 00:51:29.388
It releases a lot of glucose and at first it feels great.

1382
00:51:29.388 --> 00:51:30.580
You're woke up for the day,

1383
00:51:30.580 --> 00:51:31.910
you suddenly feel like you're awake,

1384
00:51:31.910 --> 00:51:34.040
you're alert, you're in it.

1385
00:51:34.040 --> 00:51:35.810
But what happens is you get to your school desk,

1386
00:51:35.810 --> 00:51:39.590
or your kid gets to the school desk a couple of hours later,

1387
00:51:39.590 --> 00:51:42.550
and you experience a huge energy slump.

1388
00:51:42.550 --> 00:51:44.720
Because energy's released so quickly into your brain

1389
00:51:44.720 --> 00:51:46.020
that it just crashes.

1390
00:51:46.020 --> 00:51:47.100
And when your energy crashes

1391
00:51:47.100 --> 00:51:49.120
you experience something called brain fog.

1392
00:51:49.120 --> 00:51:51.280
Brain fog is when you can't really focus and pay attention,

1393
00:51:51.280 --> 00:51:53.620
you're just very, very distractible.

1394
00:51:53.620 --> 00:51:56.150
And you need another sugary carby treat

1395
00:51:56.150 --> 00:51:57.130
to get your brain back.

1396
00:51:57.130 --> 00:51:59.720
So the way we eat puts us on a roller coaster

1397
00:51:59.720 --> 00:52:02.720
of energy spikes and energy crashes throughout the day

1398
00:52:02.720 --> 00:52:05.400
that leaves us with these patches of brain fog

1399
00:52:05.400 --> 00:52:07.250
that profoundly damage our ability to focus

1400
00:52:07.250 --> 00:52:08.320
and pay attention.

1401
00:52:08.320 --> 00:52:09.370
The way Dale Pinnock,

1402
00:52:09.370 --> 00:52:12.087
one of Britain's leading nutritionist put it to me is,

1403
00:52:12.087 --> 00:52:14.680
"It's like we put rocket fuel into a mini."

1404
00:52:14.680 --> 00:52:16.487
You know those little British cards from the 70s.

1405
00:52:16.487 --> 00:52:19.200
It will go really fast for five minutes

1406
00:52:19.200 --> 00:52:20.780
and then it just stops.

1407
00:52:20.780 --> 00:52:25.060
So if you eat food like almost all our ancestors did

1408
00:52:25.060 --> 00:52:28.250
that releases energy steadily throughout the day,

1409
00:52:28.250 --> 00:52:30.750
you will be able to pay attention much better.

1410
00:52:30.750 --> 00:52:34.370
The second way in which the way is damaging our attention

1411
00:52:34.370 --> 00:52:36.190
is that for your brain to develop fully

1412
00:52:36.190 --> 00:52:39.760
you need certain nutrients and our diets are lacking

1413
00:52:39.760 --> 00:52:40.640
a lot of those nutrients.

1414
00:52:40.640 --> 00:52:41.610
An obvious one that most people

1415
00:52:41.610 --> 00:52:43.250
will have heard of are omega-3s.

1416
00:52:43.250 --> 00:52:45.560
And it turns out supplements just don't cut it, right?

1417
00:52:45.560 --> 00:52:47.780
Your body doesn't metabolize supplements

1418
00:52:47.780 --> 00:52:50.780
in the same way it metabolizes nutrients from real food.

1419
00:52:50.780 --> 00:52:53.810
The third factor to me is actually the most disturbing,

1420
00:52:53.810 --> 00:52:56.180
which is it's not just that our diets are lacking things

1421
00:52:56.180 --> 00:52:58.230
that we need for our brains to function.

1422
00:52:58.230 --> 00:53:01.400
Our diets also currently contain chemicals

1423
00:53:01.400 --> 00:53:04.530
that act on us like drugs.

1424
00:53:04.530 --> 00:53:06.360
So there's a study in Britain

1425
00:53:06.360 --> 00:53:09.200
in a city called Southampton in 2007,

1426
00:53:09.200 --> 00:53:12.450
where they got 297 kids and they split them into two groups.

1427
00:53:12.450 --> 00:53:14.500
And the first group was just given water to drink,

1428
00:53:14.500 --> 00:53:18.510
and the second group was given water laced with food dyes

1429
00:53:18.510 --> 00:53:19.343
that occur...

1430
00:53:19.343 --> 00:53:20.440
And the kind of food we get at the supermarket

1431
00:53:20.440 --> 00:53:21.360
all the time.

1432
00:53:21.360 --> 00:53:23.530
Popular candies that kind of thing.

1433
00:53:23.530 --> 00:53:26.210
And then they monitor the kids and the kids who were given

1434
00:53:26.210 --> 00:53:29.110
the food dies were significantly more likely

1435
00:53:29.110 --> 00:53:33.030
to become hyperactive, to manic, struggle with attention.

1436
00:53:33.030 --> 00:53:35.100
And interestingly in Britain and the European Union,

1437
00:53:35.100 --> 00:53:36.840
we banned most of those food dyes.

1438
00:53:36.840 --> 00:53:38.690
In the United States there's still on the market.

1439
00:53:38.690 --> 00:53:41.280
I'm sure that's one reason why there's a significant gap

1440
00:53:41.280 --> 00:53:43.330
between the United States and Europe when it comes to things

1441
00:53:43.330 --> 00:53:45.060
like attention problems in children.

1442
00:53:45.060 --> 00:53:47.720
So you can see how those factors...

1443
00:53:47.720 --> 00:53:49.883
Again, there's a kind of doubleness to that.

1444
00:53:49.883 --> 00:53:51.880
A lot of us can change the way we eat

1445
00:53:51.880 --> 00:53:55.200
but also the food industry needs to be challenged as well.

1446
00:53:55.200 --> 00:53:56.980
So more of us can make those changes.

1447
00:53:56.980 --> 00:54:00.380
Yeah, I mean, the food thing is something we've explored

1448
00:54:00.380 --> 00:54:02.500
at length on this podcast many, many times

1449
00:54:02.500 --> 00:54:04.440
and listeners are already kind of familiar

1450
00:54:04.440 --> 00:54:05.620
with that landscape.

1451
00:54:05.620 --> 00:54:09.620
But I think when you mention the dyes that brings up

1452
00:54:10.730 --> 00:54:15.730
this subject of environmental pollutants in beauty products,

1453
00:54:15.880 --> 00:54:18.530
and things we put on our skin and how we cleanse ourselves,

1454
00:54:18.530 --> 00:54:19.750
and how we clean our houses

1455
00:54:19.750 --> 00:54:23.010
and just what is in the air in general?

1456
00:54:23.010 --> 00:54:24.230
And what's crazy...

1457
00:54:24.230 --> 00:54:28.110
I did a podcast with a woman called Gregg Renfrew,

1458
00:54:28.110 --> 00:54:31.490
who is CEO and founder of a company called Beautycounter.

1459
00:54:31.490 --> 00:54:34.940
And they have created this incredibly robust,

1460
00:54:34.940 --> 00:54:38.900
successful company that produces beauty products for women

1461
00:54:38.900 --> 00:54:40.430
that are all chemical free.

1462
00:54:40.430 --> 00:54:42.200
And she came on and she's very well versed

1463
00:54:42.200 --> 00:54:46.600
in the kind of legislative regulatory landscape

1464
00:54:46.600 --> 00:54:50.710
regarding chemicals in consumer products.

1465
00:54:50.710 --> 00:54:52.660
And it's quite shocking and you talk about this

1466
00:54:52.660 --> 00:54:53.970
in the book as well.

1467
00:54:53.970 --> 00:54:56.460
The fact that the burden of proof is reversed.

1468
00:54:56.460 --> 00:54:59.940
Like we're allowed to put all of these crazy chemicals

1469
00:54:59.940 --> 00:55:03.330
