1584
00:59:19.850 --> 00:59:24.850
That it becomes difficult to imagine that things can change.

1585
00:59:25.930 --> 00:59:28.420
When I feel like that and sometimes I do,

1586
00:59:28.420 --> 00:59:29.380
maybe this will sound odd

1587
00:59:29.380 --> 00:59:31.180
I think a lot about my grandmothers.

1588
00:59:32.090 --> 00:59:34.740
My grandmothers were the age I am now in 1963,

1589
00:59:34.740 --> 00:59:37.270
I loved my grandmothers they were amazing women.

1590
00:59:37.270 --> 00:59:39.420
They both lived to be 91.

1591
00:59:39.420 --> 00:59:42.960
And in 1963, so one of them was a working class

1592
00:59:42.960 --> 00:59:44.930
Scottish woman living in what we would call

1593
00:59:44.930 --> 00:59:47.450
a housing project here in the United States.

1594
00:59:47.450 --> 00:59:49.520
And the other was a Swiss peasant woman living

1595
00:59:49.520 --> 00:59:52.100
in a wooden hut on the side of a mountain.

1596
00:59:52.100 --> 00:59:55.580
In 1963, neither of my grandmothers were allowed

1597
00:59:55.580 --> 00:59:57.650
to have bank accounts in their own name

1598
00:59:57.650 --> 00:59:59.660
'cause their married women.

1599
00:59:59.660 --> 01:00:01.590
It was legal for their husbands to rape them

1600
01:00:01.590 --> 01:00:03.530
as it was legal everywhere in the world for men

1601
01:00:03.530 --> 01:00:04.703
to rape their wives.

1602
01:00:05.780 --> 01:00:08.020
They had both left school when they were 13,

1603
01:00:08.020 --> 01:00:09.530
even though the men in their family went on longer

1604
01:00:09.530 --> 01:00:12.130
'cause no one gave a about girls learning anything.

1605
01:00:12.130 --> 01:00:14.757
My Swiss grandmother wasn't even allowed to vote, right?

1606
01:00:14.757 --> 01:00:16.774
And I think about their lives when they're the age I am,

1607
01:00:16.774 --> 01:00:17.860
there's not some distant history.

1608
01:00:17.860 --> 01:00:18.693
I knew the...

1609
01:00:18.693 --> 01:00:20.950
I loved them and knew them incredibly well.

1610
01:00:20.950 --> 01:00:23.810
And this is when they were the age I am now.

1611
01:00:23.810 --> 01:00:25.890
And I think about the gap from then to now,

1612
01:00:25.890 --> 01:00:27.880
I don't wanna underestimate how much further we've got to go

1613
01:00:27.880 --> 01:00:29.480
in achieving equality for women.

1614
01:00:31.100 --> 01:00:33.220
But I think about my Swiss grandmother loved to paint

1615
01:00:33.220 --> 01:00:35.870
and draw when she was a teenager.

1616
01:00:35.870 --> 01:00:38.470
They told her to shut up and get into the kitchen.

1617
01:00:38.470 --> 01:00:40.280
My niece, Erin is 17,

1618
01:00:40.280 --> 01:00:42.080
she never knew my grandmother sadly.

1619
01:00:43.770 --> 01:00:44.890
When she loved to paint and draw,

1620
01:00:44.890 --> 01:00:46.907
we didn't say shut up and get into the kitchen.

1621
01:00:46.907 --> 01:00:49.823
We started looking at art schools, right?

1622
01:00:50.790 --> 01:00:53.970
When people say, "Oh, the force harming our attention

1623
01:00:53.970 --> 01:00:55.200
are big and powerful."

1624
01:00:55.200 --> 01:00:56.800
I totally agree with you.

1625
01:00:56.800 --> 01:00:58.800
I've gotta tell you they're not a 10th as powerful

1626
01:00:58.800 --> 01:01:00.910
as men were in 1963.

1627
01:01:00.910 --> 01:01:03.380
Men controlled every country in the world,

1628
01:01:03.380 --> 01:01:06.480
every company in the world, every police force.

1629
01:01:06.480 --> 01:01:07.927
And they had ever since those things

1630
01:01:07.927 --> 01:01:09.040
had been invented, right?

1631
01:01:09.040 --> 01:01:11.562
Apart from a few hereditary queens every now and then.

1632
01:01:11.562 --> 01:01:15.510
And the women of that generation did not just say,

1633
01:01:15.510 --> 01:01:18.400
oh, we're fucked then how are we ever gonna win this?

1634
01:01:18.400 --> 01:01:19.570
They started where they stood.

1635
01:01:19.570 --> 01:01:22.020
They fought every level in every office,

1636
01:01:22.020 --> 01:01:25.300
every home, at national levels.

1637
01:01:25.300 --> 01:01:27.870
And again, I don't wanna underestimate how further

1638
01:01:27.870 --> 01:01:29.530
we gotta go,

1639
01:01:29.530 --> 01:01:32.750
but what a staggering transformation now.

1640
01:01:32.750 --> 01:01:35.580
Even crazy sexist wouldn't suggest that my niece

1641
01:01:35.580 --> 01:01:37.330
it should be legal to rape her,

1642
01:01:37.330 --> 01:01:38.920
or that she shouldn't be allowed to have a bank account,

1643
01:01:38.920 --> 01:01:39.930
or she shouldn't be allowed to vote.

1644
01:01:39.930 --> 01:01:41.530
I mean, even crazy incel...

1645
01:01:41.530 --> 01:01:43.130
Maybe the craziest incel says that.

1646
01:01:43.130 --> 01:01:45.820
But you'd have to go completely outside

1647
01:01:45.820 --> 01:01:47.730
the spectrum of normal discussion, right?

1648
01:01:47.730 --> 01:01:50.730
That happened in the space of a very short period

1649
01:01:50.730 --> 01:01:52.320
of time historically, right?

1650
01:01:52.320 --> 01:01:54.810
So absolutely we can deal with these things.

1651
01:01:54.810 --> 01:01:58.200
And I argue in the book that just like we needed a need,

1652
01:01:58.200 --> 01:02:00.620
our feminist movement for women to reclaim their bodies

1653
01:02:00.620 --> 01:02:02.690
and their lives, we need an attention movement

1654
01:02:02.690 --> 01:02:04.420
to reclaim our minds, right?

1655
01:02:04.420 --> 01:02:05.904
We need to do lots of things as individuals,

1656
01:02:05.904 --> 01:02:09.140
but we also need to realize this is being done to us.

1657
01:02:09.140 --> 01:02:11.800
It is being done by really powerful forces

1658
01:02:11.800 --> 01:02:14.130
and we need to change the nature of our demand, right?

1659
01:02:14.130 --> 01:02:16.140
'Cause at the moment we're just blaming ourselves.

1660
01:02:16.140 --> 01:02:17.560
Most people just blame themselves

1661
01:02:17.560 --> 01:02:20.640
for their attention problems or ask for these

1662
01:02:20.640 --> 01:02:21.820
tiny little tweaks.

1663
01:02:21.820 --> 01:02:24.880
And I'm in favor of all the tweaks people ask for.

1664
01:02:24.880 --> 01:02:29.880
But I think we need to realize we are not medieval peasants

1665
01:02:29.920 --> 01:02:32.830
begging at the court of king Zuckerberg

1666
01:02:32.830 --> 01:02:35.690
for a few little crumbs of attention from his table, right?

1667
01:02:35.690 --> 01:02:39.200
We are the free citizens of democracies, we have dignity.

1668
01:02:39.200 --> 01:02:42.440
We own our own minds and we can take them back

1669
01:02:42.440 --> 01:02:43.530
if we want to.

1670
01:02:43.530 --> 01:02:46.440
(siren wailing)

1671
01:02:46.440 --> 01:02:48.354
(upbeat music)

1672
01:02:48.354 --> 01:02:49.187
(indistinct chatter)

1673
01:02:49.187 --> 01:02:51.770
(upbeat music)

1674
01:03:20.110 --> 01:03:23.160
So let's talk about what those systemic changes

1675
01:03:23.160 --> 01:03:26.690
would look like in the case of tech?

1676
01:03:26.690 --> 01:03:31.070
You talk about these business plan modifications.

1677
01:03:31.070 --> 01:03:36.020
Right now these platforms are driven by an ad revenue model.

1678
01:03:36.020 --> 01:03:40.230
So all the incentives are misaligned to capture

1679
01:03:40.230 --> 01:03:41.830
and monopolize your attention,

1680
01:03:41.830 --> 01:03:45.770
to collect as much data as possible, as humanly feasible,

1681
01:03:45.770 --> 01:03:49.900
as AI feasible as they can to create this avatar

1682
01:03:49.900 --> 01:03:52.300
of who you are so they can sell that information

1683
01:03:53.250 --> 01:03:57.080
to advertisers to sell you products.

1684
01:03:57.080 --> 01:04:01.790
And as a result of that we're captured by these devices

1685
01:04:01.790 --> 01:04:04.820
and then kind of sold to the highest bid.

1686
01:04:04.820 --> 01:04:09.020
So untangling that knot would require dismantling

1687
01:04:09.020 --> 01:04:13.540
that entire business model of it being driven by ad revenue

1688
01:04:13.540 --> 01:04:16.430
and creating some different version.

1689
01:04:16.430 --> 01:04:19.450
Whether it's a subscription model or whether these things

1690
01:04:19.450 --> 01:04:22.120
that have become such gigantic (indistinct)

1691
01:04:22.120 --> 01:04:25.220
should now be qualified as public utilities.

1692
01:04:25.220 --> 01:04:26.870
I think you've put that really well, Rich.

1693
01:04:26.870 --> 01:04:29.500
So this took me a long time to get my head around.

1694
01:04:29.500 --> 01:04:31.753
So I went to Silicon Valley and I interviewed lots of people

1695
01:04:31.753 --> 01:04:34.620
who had been at the heart of this machinery.

1696
01:04:34.620 --> 01:04:36.590
And I remember Tristan the moment with Tristan

1697
01:04:36.590 --> 01:04:41.453
when we were walking in San Francisco one day,

1698
01:04:42.300 --> 01:04:45.800
remember him telling me something that really haunted me.

1699
01:04:45.800 --> 01:04:46.633
So for people who don't know

1700
01:04:46.633 --> 01:04:47.770
Tristan worked at the heart of-

1701
01:04:47.770 --> 01:04:49.277
Right, we keep mentioning his name

1702
01:04:49.277 --> 01:04:50.990
but I keep thinking in the back of my mind

1703
01:04:50.990 --> 01:04:52.800
I'm not sure we've actually explained who he is

1704
01:04:52.800 --> 01:04:53.780
for people that don't know.

1705
01:04:53.780 --> 01:04:56.130
So Tristan Harris worked at the heart of Google

1706
01:04:56.130 --> 01:04:57.193
for many years.

1707
01:04:58.250 --> 01:05:00.350
And initially he worked on the Gmail team,

1708
01:05:01.400 --> 01:05:04.010
and when they were developing Gmail.

1709
01:05:04.010 --> 01:05:09.010
And one of the things they were particularly keen on doing

1710
01:05:09.450 --> 01:05:11.790
early in Gmail was getting people to use Gmail

1711
01:05:11.790 --> 01:05:12.710
more often, right?

1712
01:05:12.710 --> 01:05:14.300
Get them to pick it up more often.

1713
01:05:14.300 --> 01:05:16.550
I'll explain why in a little while.

1714
01:05:16.550 --> 01:05:18.403
One day sitting in the Googleplex,

1715
01:05:19.600 --> 01:05:22.000
and one of his colleagues said, "I've got an idea.

1716
01:05:22.000 --> 01:05:25.750
Why don't we make it so that whenever people get an email

1717
01:05:25.750 --> 01:05:27.323
their phone vibrates a little bit."

1718
01:05:27.323 --> 01:05:28.668
And (indistinct) around and said, "Oh, that's a good idea.

1719
01:05:28.668 --> 01:05:29.883
Let's do that."

1720
01:05:29.883 --> 01:05:31.840
A few days later they switched it on.

1721
01:05:31.840 --> 01:05:33.640
A week later Tristan was walking around

1722
01:05:33.640 --> 01:05:36.700
San Francisco himself and he starts hearing

1723
01:05:36.700 --> 01:05:39.493
these vibrations everywhere like a kind of bird song.

1724
01:05:40.458 --> 01:05:43.208
And he realizes, oh, we did that.

1725
01:05:44.996 --> 01:05:48.150
He realized that was happening all over the world.

1726
01:05:48.150 --> 01:05:51.110
He calculated a little while later that that decision

1727
01:05:51.110 --> 01:05:53.200
his colleague made so casually

1728
01:05:53.200 --> 01:05:57.280
was causing 10 billion interruptions in the world

1729
01:05:57.280 --> 01:05:58.840
to people's day every day.

1730
01:05:58.840 --> 01:06:00.040
Think about the switch cost effect.

1731
01:06:00.040 --> 01:06:02.370
Think about what that's doing to people's attention, right?

1732
01:06:02.370 --> 01:06:05.290
And he becomes more and more uncomfortable.

1733
01:06:05.290 --> 01:06:06.990
And he initially spoke out within Google.

1734
01:06:06.990 --> 01:06:09.959
He believed he could persuade people within Google.

1735
01:06:09.959 --> 01:06:12.850
He worked unbelievably hard at that.

1736
01:06:12.850 --> 01:06:16.160
And he was very brave in speaking out 'cause Silicon Valley

1737
01:06:16.160 --> 01:06:18.200
is full of people desperate to get into Google.

1738
01:06:18.200 --> 01:06:19.103
And here he was...

1739
01:06:19.103 --> 01:06:22.300
It's would be like an ExxonMobil exec standing up

1740
01:06:22.300 --> 01:06:23.730
in 1978 and saying,

1741
01:06:23.730 --> 01:06:26.190
hey guys, I think we're gonna melt the Arctic here, right?

1742
01:06:26.190 --> 01:06:27.950
It was incredibly brave.

1743
01:06:27.950 --> 01:06:30.540
Well, they gave him this sort of lip service role

1744
01:06:30.540 --> 01:06:31.910
as ethics officer.

1745
01:06:31.910 --> 01:06:32.883
I don't know exactly what-

1746
01:06:32.883 --> 01:06:33.716
(indistinct chatter)

1747
01:06:33.716 --> 01:06:36.443
Yeah, and he would offer up all these ideas

1748
01:06:36.443 --> 01:06:40.550
to align Google's mission with a higher ethic.

1749
01:06:40.550 --> 01:06:44.540
But obviously, all of those ideas are in counterpoint

1750
01:06:44.540 --> 01:06:46.950
to the objectives of the company itself.

1751
01:06:46.950 --> 01:06:47.783
That's exactly it.

1752
01:06:47.783 --> 01:06:50.094
What he realized was it's not that they're evil people,

1753
01:06:50.094 --> 01:06:51.562
it's not they're bad people.

1754
01:06:51.562 --> 01:06:52.720
He realized he was bumping up against

1755
01:06:52.720 --> 01:06:53.870
just a very clear logic.

1756
01:06:53.870 --> 01:06:57.335
So let's think about Facebook or TikTok or whatever one

1757
01:06:57.335 --> 01:06:59.260
we wanna talk about, right?

1758
01:06:59.260 --> 01:07:01.210
The business model is extremely simple.

1759
01:07:02.520 --> 01:07:05.690
Every time you pick up their phone and start to scroll

1760
01:07:05.690 --> 01:07:07.550
they make more money, right?

1761
01:07:07.550 --> 01:07:08.470
That's it.

1762
01:07:08.470 --> 01:07:10.594
So all of their algorithms,

1763
01:07:10.594 --> 01:07:12.510
all of their engineering genius,

1764
01:07:12.510 --> 01:07:14.052
all of these super smart people,

1765
01:07:14.052 --> 01:07:17.200
all of that human intelligence is applied towards one thing,

1766
01:07:17.200 --> 01:07:18.655
figuring out how do we get Rich to pick up

1767
01:07:18.655 --> 01:07:21.910
our app more often and how do we get him to scroll

1768
01:07:21.910 --> 01:07:23.220
as long as possible, right?

1769
01:07:23.220 --> 01:07:25.150
And how do we get Rich's kids to open the app

1770
01:07:25.150 --> 01:07:27.680
as often as possible and scroll as long as possible?

1771
01:07:27.680 --> 01:07:28.513
That's it.

1772
01:07:28.513 --> 01:07:29.600
That's how they make money.

1773
01:07:29.600 --> 01:07:31.960
Just like the head of KFC all he cares about

1774
01:07:31.960 --> 01:07:33.390
in his capacities as the head of KFC's

1775
01:07:33.390 --> 01:07:36.419
is how much fried chicken did Rich eat yesterday, right?

1776
01:07:36.419 --> 01:07:39.200
All these companies care about is how often,

1777
01:07:39.200 --> 01:07:40.710
how frequently you pick up your phone,

1778
01:07:40.710 --> 01:07:41.990
and how long you scroll.

1779
01:07:41.990 --> 01:07:45.415
So everything is designed around these two goals.

1780
01:07:45.415 --> 01:07:48.090
That's how it works.

1781
01:07:48.090 --> 01:07:49.820
But and of course, they've developed...

1782
01:07:49.820 --> 01:07:52.250
That means they've developed hundreds of techniques

1783
01:07:52.250 --> 01:07:54.290
to figure out how to hack your attention.

1784
01:07:54.290 --> 01:07:56.950
And this isn't just the view of dissidents like Tristan,

1785
01:07:56.950 --> 01:07:59.000
this is what they say, right?

1786
01:07:59.000 --> 01:08:01.190
Sean Parker, one of the biggest initial investors

1787
01:08:01.190 --> 01:08:02.117
in Facebook said,

1788
01:08:02.117 --> 01:08:05.120
"We designed it to maximally invade people's attention.

1789
01:08:05.120 --> 01:08:07.370
We knew what we were doing and we did it anyway.

1790
01:08:07.370 --> 01:08:09.830
God only knows what it's doing to our children's brains."

1791
01:08:09.830 --> 01:08:11.819
We now know the leaked data,

1792
01:08:11.819 --> 01:08:14.260
leaked internal research from Facebook

1793
01:08:14.260 --> 01:08:17.080
shows they know they're destroying our collective attention.

1794
01:08:17.080 --> 01:08:18.750
They're destroying our political attention.

1795
01:08:18.750 --> 01:08:19.730
We can talk about that more.

1796
01:08:19.730 --> 01:08:23.701
They know they're doing it and they're continuing to do it.

1797
01:08:23.701 --> 01:08:27.172
And the solution to this is you outline, Rich.

1798
01:08:27.172 --> 01:08:28.740
If that's when it was explained to me

1799
01:08:28.740 --> 01:08:30.730
it seemed almost like weirdly simple, right?

1800
01:08:30.730 --> 01:08:32.370
'Cause for so long I thought

1801
01:08:32.370 --> 01:08:34.200
oh, the problem is the existence of the smartphone,

1802
01:08:34.200 --> 01:08:36.920
and say the problem is in the existence of the smartphone.

1803
01:08:36.920 --> 01:08:40.480
The problem is that these devices are currently designed

1804
01:08:40.480 --> 01:08:42.560
to maximally invade your attention.

1805
01:08:42.560 --> 01:08:44.360
But they don't have to be designed like that.

1806
01:08:44.360 --> 01:08:46.840
Just like we have paint that doesn't have lead in it,

1807
01:08:46.840 --> 01:08:49.530
you could have social media that is not designed

1808
01:08:49.530 --> 01:08:50.930
to maximally invade your attention.

1809
01:08:50.930 --> 01:08:52.420
And I remember one of the people who really helped me

