2070
01:18:18.058 --> 01:18:21.100
It's a lot of different tiny little things that add up

2071
01:18:21.100 --> 01:18:23.060
to a very big thing.

2072
01:18:23.060 --> 01:18:26.410
So the business model, all of the...

2073
01:18:26.410 --> 01:18:27.330
All of these different things.

2074
01:18:27.330 --> 01:18:29.100
So for the average human being who's like,

2075
01:18:29.100 --> 01:18:30.220
I really don't like that.

2076
01:18:30.220 --> 01:18:32.700
I'm distracted and I'm worried about my kid.

2077
01:18:32.700 --> 01:18:35.810
And this is something worth being concerned about

2078
01:18:35.810 --> 01:18:37.650
and fighting for.

2079
01:18:37.650 --> 01:18:40.280
Like how do I even wrap my head around

2080
01:18:40.280 --> 01:18:41.250
what the solution would be?

2081
01:18:41.250 --> 01:18:44.780
Or how I get involved in participating in this?

2082
01:18:44.780 --> 01:18:45.960
Well, it's more like feminism.

2083
01:18:45.960 --> 01:18:47.163
If you think about my grandmother's, right?

2084
01:18:47.163 --> 01:18:49.620
Like I mentioned 1963.

2085
01:18:49.620 --> 01:18:53.150
My grandmother's lives were profoundly disfigured by sexism.

2086
01:18:53.150 --> 01:18:55.100
They never got to be the people they could

2087
01:18:55.100 --> 01:18:56.490
and should have been, right?

2088
01:18:56.490 --> 01:18:59.034
Because they were denied systematically opportunities

2089
01:18:59.034 --> 01:19:01.985
to develop themselves 'cause they were women.

2090
01:19:01.985 --> 01:19:06.240
And feminism, male power ran and runs through

2091
01:19:06.240 --> 01:19:08.890
the whole society at every level.

2092
01:19:08.890 --> 01:19:09.723
They were...

2093
01:19:09.723 --> 01:19:11.740
Every institution in that society

2094
01:19:11.740 --> 01:19:13.460
that my grandmothers faced.

2095
01:19:13.460 --> 01:19:17.060
But my grandmothers had a pre feminist consciousness, right?

2096
01:19:17.060 --> 01:19:19.910
They hated what had been done to them.

2097
01:19:19.910 --> 01:19:21.410
They were really pissed off.

2098
01:19:21.410 --> 01:19:24.210
Their brothers got to have decent lives and they didn't.

2099
01:19:25.340 --> 01:19:27.220
But they just thought that's the way

2100
01:19:27.220 --> 01:19:28.300
of the world, right?

2101
01:19:28.300 --> 01:19:29.133
That's just...

2102
01:19:29.133 --> 01:19:29.966
That's how it's always been.

2103
01:19:29.966 --> 01:19:31.160
That's the way of the world.

2104
01:19:31.160 --> 01:19:31.993
They didn't have a sense that this was something

2105
01:19:31.993 --> 01:19:34.520
that was done to them that could have been different.

2106
01:19:34.520 --> 01:19:36.400
And then of course we have a movement

2107
01:19:36.400 --> 01:19:38.713
that raises people's consciousness that says to them,

2108
01:19:38.713 --> 01:19:40.880
"You know, this didn't have to happen to you.

2109
01:19:40.880 --> 01:19:42.590
This wasn't inevitable, right?

2110
01:19:42.590 --> 01:19:44.847
Actually, look, we can change this if we want to."

2111
01:19:44.847 --> 01:19:47.710
And we have to challenge power all sorts of levels.

2112
01:19:47.710 --> 01:19:49.560
And I think we have to do something similar with attention.

2113
01:19:49.560 --> 01:19:51.060
So let's think about an example,

2114
01:19:51.060 --> 01:19:52.130
something I think that will really connect

2115
01:19:52.130 --> 01:19:56.320
with a lot of your listeners and viewers, Rich.

2116
01:19:56.320 --> 01:19:58.520
Let's think about the aspects of childhood, right?

2117
01:19:58.520 --> 01:20:01.250
So the last quarter of Stolen Focus is about

2118
01:20:01.250 --> 01:20:05.020
the ways in which our children's abilities focus is being

2119
01:20:05.020 --> 01:20:06.430
catastrophically degraded, right?

2120
01:20:06.430 --> 01:20:09.660
And I think most people listening can see that happening

2121
01:20:09.660 --> 01:20:12.200
around them in really disturbing ways.

2122
01:20:12.200 --> 01:20:13.920
And this is not just the same old,

2123
01:20:13.920 --> 01:20:16.360
oh, every generation thinks the next generation's

2124
01:20:16.360 --> 01:20:17.440
got a problem.

2125
01:20:17.440 --> 01:20:19.617
Professor Larry Rosen did lots of research

2126
01:20:19.617 --> 01:20:21.630
where they just look at college students,

2127
01:20:21.630 --> 01:20:22.900
these are college students.

2128
01:20:22.900 --> 01:20:24.300
And discovered they just can't focus

2129
01:20:24.300 --> 01:20:25.660
more than a few minutes, right?

2130
01:20:25.660 --> 01:20:28.840
I interviewed a professor at Harvard

2131
01:20:28.840 --> 01:20:30.939
who was talking about a specific book,

2132
01:20:30.939 --> 01:20:34.010
a little book that we were discussing.

2133
01:20:34.010 --> 01:20:36.860
And he said, "I used to give this to our students

2134
01:20:36.860 --> 01:20:38.954
to read every term.

2135
01:20:38.954 --> 01:20:39.920
And it carried on.

2136
01:20:39.920 --> 01:20:40.753
And I went later on I went back and said,

2137
01:20:40.753 --> 01:20:42.350
"Oh, why don't you give it anymore?"

2138
01:20:42.350 --> 01:20:43.767
Is that writer Thomas Kuhn is his name.

2139
01:20:43.767 --> 01:20:45.530
"Is he gone out of fashion?"

2140
01:20:45.530 --> 01:20:46.900
And he said, "No, you just...

2141
01:20:46.900 --> 01:20:48.130
They just can't read it anymore.

2142
01:20:48.130 --> 01:20:48.963
You can't give...

2143
01:20:48.963 --> 01:20:50.412
It's like 100 pages.

2144
01:20:50.412 --> 01:20:51.440
So they just won't read it.

2145
01:20:51.440 --> 01:20:53.980
We give them short articles, we give them podcasts."

2146
01:20:53.980 --> 01:20:56.820
I was like, "Shit, that's Harvard students, right?"

2147
01:20:56.820 --> 01:20:57.653
You suddenly...

2148
01:20:57.653 --> 01:20:58.486
One of those moments,

2149
01:20:58.486 --> 01:21:00.347
there's several moments where I kind of thought,

2150
01:21:00.347 --> 01:21:03.670
"Oh, shit, does this mean no one can focus anymore?"

2151
01:21:03.670 --> 01:21:05.560
If you go to places where you expect there

2152
01:21:05.560 --> 01:21:09.530
to be these pockets of focus and they aren't there.

2153
01:21:09.530 --> 01:21:11.530
So if we look at children,

2154
01:21:11.530 --> 01:21:13.040
'cause I think this is an area

2155
01:21:13.040 --> 01:21:14.760
where we could get the biggest

2156
01:21:14.760 --> 01:21:16.900
and quickest consensus on one of the changes

2157
01:21:16.900 --> 01:21:19.718
that has to happen to get our attention back.

2158
01:21:19.718 --> 01:21:21.130
I don't think it's a coincidence that we've had

2159
01:21:21.130 --> 01:21:23.333
an explosion in children's attention problems

2160
01:21:23.333 --> 01:21:26.349
at the same time as we've had a profound transformation

2161
01:21:26.349 --> 01:21:28.845
in the next of childhood itself.

2162
01:21:28.845 --> 01:21:31.590
And I tell the story of this in the book

2163
01:21:31.590 --> 01:21:33.830
through one of the heroes of the book and one of my heroes,

2164
01:21:33.830 --> 01:21:36.330
a woman called Lenore Skenazy,

2165
01:21:36.330 --> 01:21:38.210
who you should totally have on the podcast by the way.

2166
01:21:38.210 --> 01:21:40.530
So Leno grew up in Chicago...

2167
01:21:40.530 --> 01:21:43.200
In a suburb of Chicago in the 1960s.

2168
01:21:43.200 --> 01:21:45.380
And from when she was five,

2169
01:21:45.380 --> 01:21:47.750
in the morning she would leave the house on her own

2170
01:21:47.750 --> 01:21:50.880
and walk to school was about 15 minutes away.

2171
01:21:50.880 --> 01:21:52.830
And she would generally bump into the other five year olds

2172
01:21:52.830 --> 01:21:54.860
who were also walking to school on their own.

2173
01:21:54.860 --> 01:21:57.380
When they got to the school there was a 10 year old boy

2174
01:21:57.380 --> 01:21:58.890
whose job was to help the five year olds

2175
01:21:58.890 --> 01:22:00.110
across the street, right?

2176
01:22:00.110 --> 01:22:03.040
She would go to school, she would leave at three o'clock

2177
01:22:03.040 --> 01:22:05.440
and she would wander around the neighborhood on her own.

2178
01:22:05.440 --> 01:22:07.800
Play with the other kids, they'd make up games,

2179
01:22:07.800 --> 01:22:09.300
figure out whatever they wanted to do.

2180
01:22:09.300 --> 01:22:11.470
And she would find her way home at five or six

2181
01:22:11.470 --> 01:22:12.890
when she was hungry, right?

2182
01:22:12.890 --> 01:22:14.420
Now, remember that was your parents' life.

2183
01:22:14.420 --> 01:22:16.040
That was my parents' childhood, right?

2184
01:22:16.040 --> 01:22:17.730
That was mine too.

2185
01:22:17.730 --> 01:22:18.563
I'm a little...

2186
01:22:18.563 --> 01:22:19.396
I'm older than you.

2187
01:22:19.396 --> 01:22:20.229
But yeah, I would just go-

2188
01:22:20.229 --> 01:22:21.379
(indistinct chatter)

2189
01:22:21.379 --> 01:22:24.230
Leave the house in the morning and just be back

2190
01:22:24.230 --> 01:22:27.470
by dinnertime and ride my bike around and do whatever.

2191
01:22:27.470 --> 01:22:29.200
Who knows what was going on?

2192
01:22:29.200 --> 01:22:33.810
And then in the space of one generation all of that ended.

2193
01:22:33.810 --> 01:22:35.310
By the time Lenore had her own kids

2194
01:22:35.310 --> 01:22:36.310
and she was living in Queens,

2195
01:22:36.310 --> 01:22:38.410
in New York in the 1990s.

2196
01:22:38.410 --> 01:22:40.360
She was expected to walk her kids to school

2197
01:22:40.360 --> 01:22:41.600
and wait at the gate

2198
01:22:41.600 --> 01:22:43.330
when they were ready to come back, right?

2199
01:22:43.330 --> 01:22:47.910
By 2003, only 10% of American children

2200
01:22:47.910 --> 01:22:50.671
ever played outside without adult supervision.

2201
01:22:50.671 --> 01:22:51.504
And of that 10%,

2202
01:22:51.504 --> 01:22:53.634
I think they got an average of 12 minutes a week.

2203
01:22:53.634 --> 01:22:56.510
So effectively long before COVID,

2204
01:22:56.510 --> 01:22:59.370
childhood became a phenomenon that happened entirely

2205
01:22:59.370 --> 01:23:01.580
behind closed doors, right?

2206
01:23:01.580 --> 01:23:04.980
And it turns out that childhood that you had

2207
01:23:04.980 --> 01:23:08.430
contains all sorts of things that are really important

2208
01:23:08.430 --> 01:23:10.200
for children to learn attention and focus.

2209
01:23:10.200 --> 01:23:12.730
One again, it's a bit of a no Sherlock insight

2210
01:23:12.730 --> 01:23:14.400
is exercise, right?

2211
01:23:14.400 --> 01:23:15.920
Children need to run around.

2212
01:23:15.920 --> 01:23:18.500
We are the first human society ever that has tried

2213
01:23:18.500 --> 01:23:21.170
to get children to sit still for eight hours a day.

2214
01:23:21.170 --> 01:23:22.080
No one's ever tried to do it

2215
01:23:22.080 --> 01:23:24.010
'cause it's so obviously foolish.

2216
01:23:24.010 --> 01:23:25.190
The evidence is overwhelming,

2217
01:23:25.190 --> 01:23:27.070
kids who can't pay attention the single best thing

2218
01:23:27.070 --> 01:23:29.920
you can do is let them go and run around, right?

2219
01:23:29.920 --> 01:23:32.600
We've stopped children from physically roaming around.

2220
01:23:32.600 --> 01:23:34.020
But there's much more than that.

2221
01:23:34.020 --> 01:23:35.830
People like Dr. Isabel Behncke,

2222
01:23:35.830 --> 01:23:37.390
great Chilean scientist

2223
01:23:37.390 --> 01:23:40.870
have shown when children play freely with each other,

2224
01:23:40.870 --> 01:23:43.620
they learn how to deploy their attention.

2225
01:23:43.620 --> 01:23:45.530
They learn what they find interesting,

2226
01:23:45.530 --> 01:23:48.200
they learn how to make things happen with other kids,

2227
01:23:48.200 --> 01:23:50.770
they learn how to get other kids to pay attention to them,

2228
01:23:50.770 --> 01:23:53.570
how to tolerate waiting for your turn.

2229
01:23:53.570 --> 01:23:54.403
All sorts of things

2230
01:23:54.403 --> 01:23:56.870
that are completely essential for attention.

2231
01:23:56.870 --> 01:23:57.960
And we took that away.

2232
01:23:57.960 --> 01:24:00.240
Now kids do play but they play supervised

2233
01:24:00.240 --> 01:24:02.020
by adults telling them what to do.

2234
01:24:02.020 --> 01:24:02.970
And that's the equivalent.

2235
01:24:02.970 --> 01:24:05.000
It's like the demonstrating processed food

2236
01:24:05.000 --> 01:24:06.550
and whole foods, right?

2237
01:24:06.550 --> 01:24:09.290
Supervised play where adults tell them what to do

2238
01:24:09.290 --> 01:24:10.250
doesn't cut it, right?

2239
01:24:10.250 --> 01:24:12.350
Yeah, you have that example of that classroom

2240
01:24:12.350 --> 01:24:15.370
where they would allow them free time to play.

2241
01:24:15.370 --> 01:24:17.420
And the kids didn't know what to do

2242
01:24:17.420 --> 01:24:19.190
because they were waiting for somebody to tell them

2243
01:24:19.190 --> 01:24:21.020
what tat play should look.

2244
01:24:21.020 --> 01:24:22.540
It was heartbreaking.

2245
01:24:22.540 --> 01:24:23.960
But the reason why Lenore is...

2246
01:24:23.960 --> 01:24:25.020
This is not just a sad story,

2247
01:24:25.020 --> 01:24:26.950
but Lenore is one of the hair heroes of the book.

2248
01:24:26.950 --> 01:24:28.730
One of several kind of heroes who are building

2249
01:24:28.730 --> 01:24:30.720
the solutions and there's many others.

2250
01:24:30.720 --> 01:24:32.280
A guy called Andrew Barnes in New Zealand

2251
01:24:32.280 --> 01:24:34.250
who found a way to restore attention at work,

2252
01:24:34.250 --> 01:24:35.520
a whole range of people.

2253
01:24:35.520 --> 01:24:37.580
But the reason Lenore is one of the heroes

2254
01:24:37.580 --> 01:24:41.460
is because Lenore runs a group called Let Grow.

2255
01:24:41.460 --> 01:24:42.293
That is the solu...

2256
01:24:42.293 --> 01:24:43.530
I think the solution to this problem,

2257
01:24:43.530 --> 01:24:45.970
or the beginnings of the solution to this problem.

2258
01:24:45.970 --> 01:24:47.750
So Lenore was horrified by this change

2259
01:24:47.750 --> 01:24:48.590
that happened in childhood.

2260
01:24:48.590 --> 01:24:50.160
She could see why it was harmful.

2261
01:24:50.160 --> 01:24:53.420
And initially she just tried to persuade individual parents

2262
01:24:53.420 --> 01:24:54.950
to let their kids play outside.

2263
01:24:54.950 --> 01:24:58.540
So she would go to them and she would often ask them

2264
01:24:58.540 --> 01:25:01.940
a question like, "Tell me about something you loved doing

2265
01:25:01.940 --> 01:25:03.550
when you were a kid

2266
01:25:03.550 --> 01:25:05.700
that you don't allow your own child to do?"

2267
01:25:06.800 --> 01:25:08.730
And people would say, "I went into the woods,

2268
01:25:08.730 --> 01:25:10.750
I rode my bike by the clips."

2269
01:25:10.750 --> 01:25:13.040
We could all think of something, right?

2270
01:25:13.040 --> 01:25:15.720
But she found individual persuasion doesn't do it.

2271
01:25:15.720 --> 01:25:19.030
'Cause if you're the only parent letting your kid out,

2272
01:25:19.030 --> 01:25:21.310
they get frightened, you look crazy.

2273
01:25:21.310 --> 01:25:23.110
And actually sometimes people ring the police, right?

2274
01:25:23.110 --> 01:25:24.660
They see children on their own now.

2275
01:25:24.660 --> 01:25:25.520
So what she did,

2276
01:25:25.520 --> 01:25:27.112
she runs this amazing group called Let Grow,

2277
01:25:27.112 --> 01:25:28.657
a (indistinct) on every parent and grandparent

2278
01:25:28.657 --> 01:25:30.990
go to letgrow.org.

2279
01:25:30.990 --> 01:25:33.210
What they do is they go to whole schools

2280
01:25:33.210 --> 01:25:37.350
and whole communities and persuade the entire community

2281
01:25:37.350 --> 01:25:39.870
to give their children increasing levels of independence,

2282
01:25:39.870 --> 01:25:42.630
which rise up to playing freely with other kids

2283
01:25:42.630 --> 01:25:45.200
on their own out of their homes.

2284
01:25:45.200 --> 01:25:48.600
And I think of all the conversations I had for the book,

2285
01:25:48.600 --> 01:25:50.650
there were loads that were really moving.

2286
01:25:51.490 --> 01:25:53.490
I think the most moving was with a 14 year old boy

2287
01:25:53.490 --> 01:25:56.770
in Long Island, he was part of a Let Grow program.

2288
01:25:56.770 --> 01:25:59.900
So this boy, he was a big, strong boy.

2289
01:25:59.900 --> 01:26:01.923
He was taller than me, big strong boy.

2290
01:26:03.160 --> 01:26:04.870
And until this program had begun,

2291
01:26:04.870 --> 01:26:08.980
I think just over a year before he had never been allowed

2292
01:26:08.980 --> 01:26:10.420
out of his house, right?

2293
01:26:10.420 --> 01:26:11.950
On his own ever.

2294
01:26:11.950 --> 01:26:13.090
I asked him why?

2295
01:26:13.090 --> 01:26:14.610
His parents didn't even let him go for a jog

2296
01:26:14.610 --> 01:26:15.760
around the block.

2297
01:26:15.760 --> 01:26:17.277
I asked him why and he said,

2298
01:26:17.277 --> 01:26:18.110
"My parent...

2299
01:26:18.110 --> 01:26:18.943
I'll never forget this phrase.

2300
01:26:18.943 --> 01:26:22.130
He said, "My parents were afraid of all these kidnappings."

2301
01:26:22.130 --> 01:26:22.963
To give you a sense,

2302
01:26:22.963 --> 01:26:26.460
this boy he lives in a town where the olive oil stores

2303
01:26:26.460 --> 01:26:28.920
across the street from the French bakery, right?

2304
01:26:28.920 --> 01:26:30.730
And he had a level of fear that would be appropriate

2305
01:26:30.730 --> 01:26:31.863
if he lived in Syria.

2306
01:26:32.860 --> 01:26:35.000
And then this program began

2307
01:26:35.000 --> 01:26:37.250
and he started to play outside his home with his friends

2308
01:26:37.250 --> 01:26:38.470
and asked him how it felt

2309
01:26:38.470 --> 01:26:40.270
to just be out of his home?

2310
01:26:40.270 --> 01:26:42.800
And he described how they played with each other

2311
01:26:42.800 --> 01:26:45.310
and then they went into the woods

2312
01:26:45.310 --> 01:26:47.560
and they built a fort together.

2313
01:26:47.560 --> 01:26:48.393
And he said...

2314
01:26:48.393 --> 01:26:49.226
With real shock, he said,

2315
01:26:49.226 --> 01:26:50.570
"Our cell phones don't work in the woods

2316
01:26:50.570 --> 01:26:51.946
so we still go there."

2317
01:26:51.946 --> 01:26:53.104
(Johann and Rich chuckling)

2318
01:26:53.104 --> 01:26:54.000
As if that was some mind blowing thing.

2319
01:26:54.000 --> 01:26:55.893
And watching this boy speak,

2320
01:26:57.330 --> 01:26:58.750
maybe it sounds melodramatic,

2321
01:26:58.750 --> 01:27:00.520
but it really did feel like watching a child

2322
01:27:00.520 --> 01:27:02.490
come to life, right?

2323
01:27:02.490 --> 01:27:03.530
I thought about all the kids

2324
01:27:03.530 --> 01:27:06.630
I know who never get to go out and do anything.

2325
01:27:06.630 --> 01:27:08.870
The only place we let our children explore anything

2326
01:27:08.870 --> 01:27:10.480
is on Fortnite or World of Warcraft.

2327
01:27:10.480 --> 01:27:12.560
We can hardly be surprised they become obsessed with them.

2328
01:27:12.560 --> 01:27:15.080
And Lenore was with me that day when I spoke to that boy

2329
01:27:15.080 --> 01:27:18.197
and when he left she turned to me and she said,

2330
01:27:18.197 --> 01:27:21.870
"Think about human history, all of human history,

2331
01:27:21.870 --> 01:27:25.310
young people had to go out and explore.

2332
01:27:25.310 --> 01:27:27.900
They had to hide, they had to seek, they had to hunt,

2333
01:27:27.900 --> 01:27:30.220
they had to build things.

2334
01:27:30.220 --> 01:27:32.010
And then we took all that away from them."

2335
01:27:32.010 --> 01:27:35.200
And those boys given just a tiny little sliver of freedom,

2336
01:27:35.200 --> 01:27:36.033
what did they do?

2337
01:27:36.033 --> 01:27:37.290
They went and built a fort.

2338
01:27:37.290 --> 01:27:39.930
This is so deep in human nature.

2339
01:27:39.930 --> 01:27:42.470
Now I would argue every single school in the United States

2340
01:27:42.470 --> 01:27:43.840
should ever Let Grow program.

2341
01:27:43.840 --> 01:27:46.680
This costs nothing it's free, right?

2342
01:27:46.680 --> 01:27:49.460
It's one of the lowest hanging fruit things

2343
01:27:49.460 --> 01:27:51.290
we can do to restore attention.

2344
01:27:51.290 --> 01:27:52.490
And it's particularly important

2345
01:27:52.490 --> 01:27:55.290
'cause if kids don't learn to pay attention,

2346
01:27:55.290 --> 01:27:56.490
they're gonna struggle their whole lives with it.

2347
01:27:56.490 --> 01:27:58.060
If you don't learn it in childhood and adolescents,

2348
01:27:58.060 --> 01:27:59.700
it's gonna get much harder for you.

2349
01:27:59.700 --> 01:28:01.300
But we can hardly be surprised our kids

2350
01:28:01.300 --> 01:28:02.660
are surprised with their devices.

2351
01:28:02.660 --> 01:28:05.060
If that's the only interesting thing in their life,

2352
01:28:05.060 --> 01:28:08.210
if that's the only zone of freedom they ever get, right?

2353
01:28:08.210 --> 01:28:11.060
So I think we've really got to do this.

2354
01:28:11.060 --> 01:28:12.262
Does that ring true to you, Rich?

2355
01:28:12.262 --> 01:28:13.610
Yeah, 100%.

2356
01:28:13.610 --> 01:28:18.610
I mean, I think that there's that narrow window of time

2357
01:28:19.260 --> 01:28:22.590
where brain plasticity is at it's peak

2358
01:28:22.590 --> 01:28:25.750
and that's the same moment where these young people

2359
01:28:25.750 --> 01:28:29.930
are enuring themselves to habits around technology

2360
01:28:29.930 --> 01:28:34.930
that are basically destroying that ability to concentrate

2361
01:28:35.000 --> 01:28:36.790
before they even get out of the gate

2362
01:28:36.790 --> 01:28:39.210
and have opportunities to deploy it.

2363
01:28:39.210 --> 01:28:44.210
And I think in the case that you just explained,

2364
01:28:45.400 --> 01:28:48.150
what I was thinking about was the broader conversation

2365
01:28:48.150 --> 01:28:51.870
and issue which is this fear-based society

2366
01:28:51.870 --> 01:28:54.220
that is driven by media and technology

2367
01:28:54.220 --> 01:28:58.740
because those platforms are incentivized to provoke fear.

2368
01:28:58.740 --> 01:29:01.930
So you have a kid who's afraid to go outside,

2369
01:29:01.930 --> 01:29:03.980
whose relationship with the outside world

2370
01:29:03.980 --> 01:29:06.150
is that of somebody who lives in Syria,

2371
01:29:06.150 --> 01:29:09.080
who has an irrational fear of being kidnapped

2372
01:29:09.080 --> 01:29:13.760
driven by his parents I'm sure telling him it's not safe.

2373
01:29:13.760 --> 01:29:14.890
So where is that coming from?

2374
01:29:14.890 --> 01:29:17.723
Well, that's coming from media and technology, right?

2375
01:29:18.580 --> 01:29:21.980
So the downstream implications of the at is that

2376
01:29:21.980 --> 01:29:24.297
the kid has an unhealthy relationship with the world.

2377
01:29:24.297 --> 01:29:28.480
And I think all of this is being fomented by COVID,

2378
01:29:28.480 --> 01:29:31.370
which is enhancing this idea that the world

2379
01:29:31.370 --> 01:29:32.860
is a scary place.

2380
01:29:32.860 --> 01:29:34.100
That if you go outside

2381
01:29:34.100 --> 01:29:36.090
and you're not wearing a mask you could die.

2382
01:29:36.090 --> 01:29:39.923
Like for a young person that relationship

2383
01:29:39.923 --> 01:29:44.923
with the external world is really off the rails right now

2384
01:29:45.850 --> 01:29:47.130
in so many ways.

2385
01:29:47.130 --> 01:29:49.803
And a lot of your book was written kind of...

2386
01:29:49.803 --> 01:29:53.520
You've put pandemic pieces into it.

2387
01:29:53.520 --> 01:29:56.290
But really my sense was that the gravamen of it

2388
01:29:56.290 --> 01:30:00.450
was researched and written in kind of a pre pandemic state.

2389
01:30:00.450 --> 01:30:04.070
I feel like all of these issues that you address in the book

2390
01:30:04.070 --> 01:30:06.840
are really even further complicated

2391
01:30:06.840 --> 01:30:09.130
by what we've experienced these last two years.

2392
01:30:09.130 --> 01:30:10.369
It really prepared me...

2393
01:30:10.369 --> 01:30:11.467
Obviously, the last bit of the book is about COVID.

2394
01:30:11.467 --> 01:30:14.130
But it really prepared me...

2395
01:30:14.130 --> 01:30:16.190
I think the research I did before prepared me

2396
01:30:16.190 --> 01:30:17.400
to understand something that happened

2397
01:30:17.400 --> 01:30:19.290
to all of us under COVID.

2398
01:30:19.290 --> 01:30:21.393
So I remember at the start of the pandemic,

2399
01:30:21.393 --> 01:30:23.270
I was actually ill at the start of the pandemic.

2400
01:30:23.270 --> 01:30:24.994
I caught COVID before it was fashionable.

2401
01:30:24.994 --> 01:30:28.970
And I remember very early lots of people saying...

2402
01:30:28.970 --> 01:30:31.750
Those of us who did not have to do the heroic work

2403
01:30:31.750 --> 01:30:34.070
of emergency workers and so on.

2404
01:30:34.070 --> 01:30:36.830
Saying, "Oh, we're gonna be shot in our homes for a while.

2405
01:30:36.830 --> 01:30:37.940
I'm gonna read Todd's Story,

2406
01:30:37.940 --> 01:30:40.050
I'm gonna learn French on Duolingo.

2407
01:30:40.050 --> 01:30:42.050
And you wanna noticed no one read Todd's Story,

2408
01:30:42.050 --> 01:30:43.440
no one learned French, right?

2409
01:30:43.440 --> 01:30:45.590
In fact, people Googling how do I get my brain to work

2410
01:30:45.590 --> 01:30:47.870
increased by more than 300%.

2411
01:30:47.870 --> 01:30:51.259
And I think there's a reason why there is...

2412
01:30:51.259 --> 01:30:52.092
There are many reasons why.

2413
01:30:52.092 --> 01:30:52.925
But I think one reason

2414
01:30:52.925 --> 01:30:54.730
why I think is really worth thinking about.

2415
01:30:54.730 --> 01:30:57.220
so I had interviewed...

2416
01:30:57.220 --> 01:30:58.053
And I was stress...

2417
01:30:58.053 --> 01:30:58.886
she was not talking about COVID.

2418
01:30:58.886 --> 01:30:59.764
This is before COVID.

2419
01:30:59.764 --> 01:31:01.480
But I interviewed Dr. Nadine Burke Harris,

2420
01:31:01.480 --> 01:31:03.680
who's now the surgeon general of California.

2421
01:31:05.107 --> 01:31:07.070
And she said something to me that really helped me

2422
01:31:07.070 --> 01:31:08.570
to understand this.

2423
01:31:08.570 --> 01:31:11.569
She she's done this huge amount of work on the research

2424
01:31:11.569 --> 01:31:16.569
on the ways in which stress alters attention.

2425
01:31:16.760 --> 01:31:19.930
And she said to me, "Imagine one day you're walking down

2426
01:31:19.930 --> 01:31:21.610
the street and out of the blue

2427
01:31:21.610 --> 01:31:25.480
you were attacked by a bear and you survive.

2428
01:31:25.480 --> 01:31:28.030
In the weeks and months that follow something completely

2429
01:31:28.030 --> 01:31:30.340
involuntary will happen to your attention."

2430
01:31:30.340 --> 01:31:32.450
You're gonna find it harder to say read a book

2431
01:31:32.450 --> 01:31:33.810
because part of your brain will just be

2432
01:31:33.810 --> 01:31:36.250
scanning the horizon for dangers, right?

2433
01:31:36.250 --> 01:31:37.810
Something came out the blue.

2434
01:31:37.810 --> 01:31:39.610
So what else is gonna come out the blue and attack me?

2435
01:31:39.610 --> 01:31:41.080
So your brain is scanning.

2436
01:31:41.080 --> 01:31:42.410
It's called vigilance, right?

2437
01:31:42.410 --> 01:31:45.280
Your brain is more vigilant to risk and danger.

2438
01:31:45.280 --> 01:31:48.030
Okay, now imagine the bear attacks you again.

2439
01:31:48.030 --> 01:31:51.150
A bear comes back and attacks you again, right?

2440
01:31:51.150 --> 01:31:51.987
Likely in the...

2441
01:31:51.987 --> 01:31:53.560
And you survive again.

2442
01:31:53.560 --> 01:31:56.240
In the aftermath of that you're likely to go

2443
01:31:56.240 --> 01:31:58.230
into a state called hypervigilance.

2444
01:31:58.230 --> 01:32:00.030
hypervigilance is where you can't focus on things

2445
01:32:00.030 --> 01:32:01.170
that are in front of you

2446
01:32:01.170 --> 01:32:02.003
'cause you're just...

2447
01:32:02.003 --> 01:32:04.360
Your brain is so primed for risk and danger.

2448
01:32:04.360 --> 01:32:05.797
Kids who've been through severe trauma very often

2449
01:32:05.797 --> 01:32:07.797
are in a state of hypervigilance, right?

2450
01:32:10.210 --> 01:32:12.950
There was a child psychiatrist in Australia in Adelaide,

2451
01:32:12.950 --> 01:32:14.850
called Dr Jon Jureidini.

2452
01:32:14.850 --> 01:32:16.606
Who said something to me about this to help me

2453
01:32:16.606 --> 01:32:17.439
to think about this.

2454
01:32:17.439 --> 01:32:21.880
He said, "Deep focus is a really good strategy

2455
01:32:21.880 --> 01:32:23.290
when you're safe."

2456
01:32:23.290 --> 01:32:24.830
Sit down and read a book, you'll grow,

2457
01:32:24.830 --> 01:32:27.420
you'll know more stuff you'll be smarter.

2458
01:32:27.420 --> 01:32:29.780
Deep focus is a really dumb strategy if you're in danger,

2459
01:32:29.780 --> 01:32:32.620
you'd be fool to sit at the Battle of the Somme

2460
01:32:32.620 --> 01:32:35.230
reading a novel you're gonna be shot, right?

2461
01:32:35.230 --> 01:32:40.230
So when we're plunged into unsafe situations

2462
01:32:40.400 --> 01:32:42.180
our attention will flip, right?

2463
01:32:42.180 --> 01:32:43.470
It's not that you're not paying attention

2464
01:32:43.470 --> 01:32:46.390
but you're paying attention to the detection of risk.

2465
01:32:46.390 --> 01:32:48.180
And I think in the situation when with COVID,

2466
01:32:48.180 --> 01:32:49.013
there's lots of things that have happened

2467
01:32:49.013 --> 01:32:53.640
that affected our attention but the bear came back, right?

2468
01:32:53.640 --> 01:32:55.324
The bear came back twice more times.

2469
01:32:55.324 --> 01:32:58.630
I think anyone who's struggling to focus

2470
01:32:58.630 --> 01:33:01.520
in a global pandemic I would say don't...

2471
01:33:01.520 --> 01:33:03.180
it's not your fault, right?

2472
01:33:03.180 --> 01:33:04.013
You actually...

2473
01:33:04.013 --> 01:33:06.350
What your brain is doing is trying to protect you from risk.

2474
01:33:06.350 --> 01:33:07.340
There's the risk of the virus,

2475
01:33:07.340 --> 01:33:09.010
there's the risk of the changes to our lives

2476
01:33:09.010 --> 01:33:10.440
which have been unprecedented

2477
01:33:10.440 --> 01:33:12.588
and extremely weird and disorientating.

2478
01:33:12.588 --> 01:33:16.330
There's the risk of our sense of the future

2479
01:33:16.330 --> 01:33:18.430
being profoundly disrupted.

2480
01:33:18.430 --> 01:33:19.870
There's lots of other factors that have played out

2481
01:33:19.870 --> 01:33:21.223
as well that we can talk about.

2482
01:33:21.223 --> 01:33:23.690
So I think what's happened is we've been plunged

2483
01:33:23.690 --> 01:33:25.000
into a state of hypervigilance.

2484
01:33:25.000 --> 01:33:27.030
I also think that can help us to understand

2485
01:33:27.030 --> 01:33:29.390
more widely about stress...

2486
01:33:29.390 --> 01:33:34.390
What stress in more normal times does to our focus.

2487
01:33:34.690 --> 01:33:38.070
Stress really damages your ability to pay attention

2488
01:33:38.070 --> 01:33:39.743
which is why I go through lots of things in the book

2489
01:33:39.743 --> 01:33:42.180
that we can do to reduce stress us both individually,

2490
01:33:42.180 --> 01:33:45.290
and collectively because anything

2491
01:33:45.290 --> 01:33:48.193
that reduces stress improves the ability to focus deeply.

2492
01:33:50.090 --> 01:33:51.039
Yeah.

2493
01:33:51.039 --> 01:33:51.872
Can I mention something

2494
01:33:51.872 --> 01:33:52.705
you mentioned before though, Rich about...

2495
01:33:52.705 --> 01:33:54.590
Could you come back to you was another thing you said about

2496
01:33:54.590 --> 01:33:57.320
the way technology encourages this fear?

2497
01:33:57.320 --> 01:33:58.600
Do you mind if I talk a little bit about that?

2498
01:33:58.600 --> 01:33:59.760
Yeah.

2499
01:33:59.760 --> 01:34:00.593
'Cause that...

2500
01:34:00.593 --> 01:34:02.790
It's really important to understand this.

2501
01:34:02.790 --> 01:34:05.000
Of all the things I learned this to me

2502
01:34:05.000 --> 01:34:06.203
was one of the most revelatory

2503
01:34:06.203 --> 01:34:08.167
and helped me to understand a lot of the things

2504
01:34:08.167 --> 01:34:10.620
that have happened in the world in the last five years.

2505
01:34:10.620 --> 01:34:15.150
So this to a large degree, not entirely,

2506
01:34:15.150 --> 01:34:18.110
but to a large degree is the result of what we were talking

2507
01:34:18.110 --> 01:34:19.840
about in relation to the business model.

2508
01:34:19.840 --> 01:34:23.357
So we've got a business model where the algorithm

2509
01:34:23.357 --> 01:34:25.930
are all primed to figure out what will keep

2510
01:34:25.930 --> 01:34:27.550
Rich scrolling, right?

2511
01:34:27.550 --> 01:34:28.383
That's it.

2512
01:34:28.383 --> 01:34:29.870
That's all the algorithm is detecting.

2513
01:34:29.870 --> 01:34:31.430
Constantly figuring it out.

2514
01:34:31.430 --> 01:34:33.630
What kind of posts keep Rich scrolling,

2515
01:34:33.630 --> 01:34:37.130
and what kind of posts make Rich put down the app, right?

2516
01:34:37.130 --> 01:34:39.270
Constantly figuring that out.

2517
01:34:39.270 --> 01:34:41.170
And unfortunately, and this was not to be fair to them.

2518
01:34:41.170 --> 01:34:45.010
The intention of anyone at Facebook, TikTok and these sites,

2519
01:34:45.010 --> 01:34:47.720
those algorithms bumped into

2520
01:34:47.720 --> 01:34:49.740
a deep human psychological truth.

2521
01:34:49.740 --> 01:34:51.110
That's actually been known about by psychologists

2522
01:34:51.110 --> 01:34:52.330
more than 100 years.

2523
01:34:52.330 --> 01:34:53.690
Which is called negativity bias.

2524
01:34:53.690 --> 01:34:54.890
It's very simple.

2525
01:34:54.890 --> 01:34:58.180
You will stare longer as something that makes you angry

2526
01:34:58.180 --> 01:35:00.070
and upset than you will at something

2527
01:35:00.070 --> 01:35:01.690
that makes you feel good.

2528
01:35:01.690 --> 01:35:04.310
Anyone who's ever seen a car crash on the highway

2529
01:35:04.310 --> 01:35:05.440
knows how that works.

2530
01:35:05.440 --> 01:35:07.230
You stared longer at the mango car wreck

2531
01:35:07.230 --> 01:35:08.870
than you did at the pretty flowers

2532
01:35:08.870 --> 01:35:10.180
on the other side of the street.

2533
01:35:10.180 --> 01:35:11.770
This is very deep in human nature.

2534
01:35:11.770 --> 01:35:14.670
Eight week old babies will stare longer

2535
01:35:14.670 --> 01:35:16.740
at an angry face than a happy face.

2536
01:35:16.740 --> 01:35:19.260
It's probably for a very good reason in our evolution.

2537
01:35:19.260 --> 01:35:22.040
Our ancestors who were looking out for risk,

2538
01:35:22.040 --> 01:35:24.046
got to be our ancestors and the other ones got eaten.

2539
01:35:24.046 --> 01:35:25.663
That's a slightly crude way of putting it

2540
01:35:25.663 --> 01:35:26.650
but you know what I mean?

2541
01:35:26.650 --> 01:35:28.910
But when you have a business model designed

2542
01:35:28.910 --> 01:35:32.600
to keep people scrolling that bumps into negativity bias,

2543
01:35:32.600 --> 01:35:33.433
it discovers...

2544
01:35:33.433 --> 01:35:35.380
It produces a horrific outcome.

2545
01:35:35.380 --> 01:35:39.340
So let's imagine two teenage girls who go to the same party

2546
01:35:39.340 --> 01:35:41.740
and leave to go home on the same bus.

2547
01:35:41.740 --> 01:35:44.860
And one of them does a status update where she says,

2548
01:35:44.860 --> 01:35:46.160
that was a great party.

2549
01:35:46.160 --> 01:35:47.827
I had a great time, everyone looked good.

2550
01:35:47.827 --> 01:35:49.880
And the other teenage girl goes,

2551
01:35:49.880 --> 01:35:51.890
Karen was a fucking skank at that party

2552
01:35:51.890 --> 01:35:52.859
and her boyfriend's a prick.

2553
01:35:52.859 --> 01:35:53.692
(Rich laughs)

2554
01:35:53.692 --> 01:35:55.950
And just as a rant against everyone there, right?

2555
01:35:55.950 --> 01:35:56.950
The algorithm scans,

2556
01:35:56.950 --> 01:36:00.290
it looks of words relating to anger, and outrage.

2557
01:36:00.290 --> 01:36:02.980
It'll put the first update into some people's feed,

2558
01:36:02.980 --> 01:36:06.200
it'll put the second update into way more people's feed

2559
01:36:06.200 --> 01:36:08.620
because it knows if it's enraging, it's engaging,

2560
01:36:08.620 --> 01:36:10.130
it will keep people scrolling.

2561
01:36:10.130 --> 01:36:11.034
More people go,

2562
01:36:11.034 --> 01:36:11.867
what do you mean Karen is skank?

2563
01:36:11.867 --> 01:36:12.971
You're a skank.

2564
01:36:12.971 --> 01:36:14.150
You can see how conversation will go.

2565
01:36:14.150 --> 01:36:16.730
Now that is bad enough at the level of teenage girls on...

2566
01:36:16.730 --> 01:36:19.350
We can see what's happening to teenage girls mental health.

2567
01:36:19.350 --> 01:36:21.810
Professor Jill Twenge has done very good research...

2568
01:36:21.810 --> 01:36:23.010
Sorry, I've said her name wrong.

2569
01:36:23.010 --> 01:36:26.330
Professor Jean Twenge has done very good research on this.

2570
01:36:26.330 --> 01:36:30.970
But think about that effect when it's applied

2571
01:36:30.970 --> 01:36:33.740
to our whole society, right?

2572
01:36:33.740 --> 01:36:36.173
When we are all plugged into machinery

2573
01:36:36.173 --> 01:36:40.300
that makes us angrier and angrier angrier.

2574
01:36:40.300 --> 01:36:41.430
We know what the effect is

2575
01:36:41.430 --> 01:36:42.330
'cause we just have to turn on

2576
01:36:42.330 --> 01:36:43.470
the news, right?

2577
01:36:43.470 --> 01:36:46.300
We've become a society where we can't listen to each other,

2578
01:36:46.300 --> 01:36:48.570
where we are constantly screaming at each other,

2579
01:36:48.570 --> 01:36:50.072
where everyone is...

2580
01:36:50.072 --> 01:36:53.280
The center is collapsing and people are going to greater,

2581
01:36:53.280 --> 01:36:54.730
and greater extremes.

2582
01:36:54.730 --> 01:36:57.530
When Brazil elected a far right president,

2583
01:36:57.530 --> 01:37:00.140
